tel 1.0

base anthology
import soundness

info   compilers and linkers as values: Scala, Java and Kotlin in-process, with JVM, JavaScript, Wasm, native and Android outputs
detail
    _Anthology_ drives compilers and linkers programmatically, as typed values rather than
    command lines. A compiler — `Scalac`, `Javac` or `Kotlinc` — is a value carrying typed
    options, valid only for the language versions which accept them; it compiles a map of
    named sources against a `LocalClasspath` into a directory, and hands back a
    `Compiler.Process` whose diagnostics (`Notice`s with their importance, file and span),
    progress and `Compiler.Result` are values, not console output to re-parse. A crash is a
    `Compiler.Result.Crash` with its stack trace, and a running compilation can be aborted.

    Linking is the second half. The intermediate representation a compilation emits is a
    `Universe` — `Classfile`, `Sjsir` or `Nir` — and the things a build produces are
    `Artifact.Application` nodes: an executable `Artifact.Jar`, a `Library` JAR, JavaScript
    (`anthology.Js`) under a chosen module system, browser Wasm (`anthology.Wasm`), a WASI
    component (`Wasi`), an `OciImage` wrapping one, a native `Artifact.Binary` per `Triple`,
    a `Dex` archive and a signed `Apk`. Universes, languages and applications are the nodes of
    a `Toolchain`, a directed acyclic graph whose edges are tools; producing one format from
    another is path search over it, so a multi-stage build (classfiles dexed, then packaged
    as an APK; a component linked, then wrapped as an OCI artifact) composes from the edges
    without any bespoke pipeline.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
    import logging.silentLogging
    import probates.cancelProbate
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import systems.javaBaseSystem
    import threads.virtualThreads
    import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

    val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
    val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]
    val source = t"object Main:\n  def main(args: scala.Array[String]): Unit = println(\"hello\")\n"

    supervise:
      val compiler = Scalac[3.8](List(scalacOptions.newSyntax, scalacOptions.warnings.deprecation))
      val process = compiler(classpath)(Map(t"hello.scala" -> source), out)
      val result: Compiler.Result = process.complete()

      process.notices.each: notice =>
        println(t"${notice.file}: ${notice.message}")

      Toolchain(jarEdges()).produce
        ( Deliverable.Emission(out, classpath),
          Universe.Classfile,
          Artifact.Jar,
          out,
          List(jarOptions.name(t"app.jar")),
          List(EntryPoint(Fqcn(t"Main"))) )
    ```

    The library is split into components by the tool each one wraps, so that a build takes on
    only the dependencies it uses:

    - `anthology.core` — the vocabulary: `Compiler`, `Compiler.Process`, `Notice`, `Importance`,
      `Compiler.Result`, `Compiler.Event`, `Artifact`, `Universe`, `Deliverable`, `Edge`, `Tool`,
      `Toolchain`, `EntryPoint`, `Link`, `LinkEvent`, `Triple`, `NirPlugin` and the two
      edgeless nodes `Component` and `WasmObject`. JVM only (it needs hellenism's classpath).
    - `anthology.scala` — `Scalac`, its `scalacOptions`, warm `Scalac.Session`s, and the
      `scalacEdges` compile edges. The Scala compiler (`scala3-compiler`) is a *compile-only*
      dependency: the program which drives it puts the compiler it wants on its own runtime
      classpath, and `Scalac.version` reports which one that was.
    - `anthology.java` — `Javac` and `javacEdges`, over the running JDK's own compiler.
    - `anthology.kotlin` — `Kotlinc`, `kotlincOptions` and `kotlincEdges`, over
      `kotlin-compiler-embeddable`, again compile-only: the caller supplies the compiler.
    - `anthology.linker` — `jarEdges`, `jarOptions`, `Library` and `Bundler`: JAR packaging,
      which needs no external tool.
    - `anthology.link` — `sjsEdges`, `linkerOptions`, `Wasi`, `WasiToolchain` (and the
      unexported `anthology.Js` and `anthology.Wasm` nodes), driving the Scala.js linker, a JVM
      library bundled with the component. A WASI component link additionally shells out to
      `wasm-tools` and the scala-wasm fork of `wit-bindgen`, which `WasiToolchain()` probes for.
    - `anthology.nir` — `nativeEdges` and `nativeOptions`, driving the Scala Native build
      (`tools_3`, bundled) through `clang` and `clang++`, which `nativeEdges()` probes for on
      the `PATH`. Compiling *into* `Universe.Nir` needs the Scala Native compiler plugin, named
      by a `NirPlugin` given.
    - `anthology.dex` — `Dex`, `dexEdges` and `dexOptions`, driving R8's D8 dexer (bundled).
    - `anthology.apk` — `Apk`, `apkEdges`, `apkOptions` and `Axml`: a complete, signed,
      zip-aligned Android package with no Android SDK build tool at all; only a keystore.
    - `anthology.oci` — `OciImage`, `ociEdges`, `ociOptions` and `OciConfiguration`: a WASI
      component wrapped as a Wasm OCI artifact.

    Every component is JVM-only, and everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella except
    three names which would collide there: `anthology.Js` and `anthology.Wasm` (the umbrella's
    `Js` and `Wasm` are xenophile's, the JavaScript and Wasm foreign-function interfaces) and
    `anthology.Language` (the umbrella's is cosmopolite's). Under `import soundness.*` these
    three are written fully qualified, `anthology.Js(anthology.Js.Module.Es)`. The option
    objects `scalacOptions` and `kotlincOptions` are mirrored as packages of the umbrella, so
    `scalacOptions.experimental` reads the same way from either.

    The contextual values a compile needs are a `System` (`systems.javaBaseSystem`), a
    `Monitor` (inside `supervise`, with a `threads` given) and a `Probate`
    (`probates.cancelProbate`), a `Compiler.Event is Loggable` (`logging.silentLogging` to
    discard the events) and a `Tactic[Compiler.Error]`; a `Toolchain#produce` needs a
    `Monitor`, a `System`, a `WorkingDirectory`, a `Tactic[Link.Error]` and a `LinkEvent is
    Loggable`. The tool-probing constructors `WasiToolchain()` and `nativeEdges()` also need a
    `WorkingDirectory` and an `Environment`.

refer anthology.Compiler
refer anthology.Scalac
refer anthology.Compiler.Process
refer anthology.Toolchain
refer anthology.Artifact
refer anthology.Universe

entry .Compiler   the shape of an in-process compiler: a version, and sources plus classpath to a `Compiler.Process`
  detail
      `Compiler` is the interface `Scalac`, `Javac` and `Kotlinc` all present: `#version`
      reports the compiler the value drives, as the compiler itself reports it, and `#apply`
      compiles a map of named sources against a `LocalClasspath` into an output directory,
      returning a `Process` whose diagnostics and result arrive as values. Each
      compiler takes the same arguments in the same order, so a tool which orchestrates
      compilation does not change idiom per language.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import logging.silentLogging
      import probates.cancelProbate
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]

      supervise:
        val process = Javac(Nil)(classpath)(Map(t"Hello.java" -> t"class Hello {}"), out)
        process.complete()
      ```

      The companion holds `Compiler.Error`, the one error every compiler raises when it cannot
      be configured at all, as distinct from a compilation which runs and fails.
  refer anthology.Scalac
  refer anthology.Javac
  refer anthology.Kotlinc
  refer anthology.Compiler.Process
  entry .Error   raised when a compiler cannot be set up from its options
    detail
        Error 779: _there was a problem with the compiler configuration_. Raised when the
        compiler's base context cannot be built from the arguments the `Scalac` was given —
        an option the embedded compiler rejects — which happens when a `Scalac.Session` is
        opened. It is a configuration fault, not a compilation failure: sources which do not
        compile give a `Result.Failure` with `Notice`s, and a compiler which throws
        gives a `Result.Crash`, neither of which raises this.
  entry #version   the version of the compiler this value drives, as the compiler reports it
      `3.10.0` for an embedded Scala compiler, `25.0.4` for the running JDK's Java compiler,
      `2.4.10` for the Kotlin compiler on the classpath. A build records it as the tool which
      produced an output, and a memoization key must include it so that a rebuild misses when
      the compiler changes. The `Process` a compile returns carries the same text as
      its own `version`.
  entry #apply   compiles named sources against a classpath into a directory, as a `Compiler.Process`
      `compiler(classpath)(sources, out)`: the sources are a `Map` from a name (which the
      diagnostics will cite, `hello.scala`) to its content, the classpath a `LocalClasspath`
      the sources compile against, and `out` any path type `Abstractable across Paths to
      Text`. It needs a `System` and a `Monitor`, logs `Compiler.Event`s, and raises
      `Compiler.Error`. The output directory must already exist for `Scalac`; the compile
      edges of a `Toolchain` create it, but a direct call does not.

  entry .Process   a running or finished compilation: its diagnostics, progress and result as values
    detail
        Every compiler's `apply` returns a `Process` at once, while the compilation runs
        in a task of its own (`Scalac`, `Javac`, `Kotlinc`) or has already completed (a
        `Scalac.Session.Process`). `#complete()` awaits the `Result`; `#notices` is the
        list of diagnostics so far, oldest first; `#errors` and `#warnings` count them; and
        `#updates` is a live feed interleaving diagnostics and progress ticks in arrival order,
        for a tool which shows a compilation as it happens. `#abort()` asks the compiler to
        stop at its next progress tick.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import logging.silentLogging
        import probates.cancelProbate
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import systems.javaBaseSystem
        import threads.virtualThreads

        val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
        val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]

        supervise:
          val process = Scalac[3.8](Nil)(classpath)(Map(t"bad.scala" -> t"class Bad:\n  def x: Int = \"nope\"\n"), out)

          process.complete() match
            case Compiler.Result.Success      => t"compiled"
            case Compiler.Result.Failure      => t"${process.errors} errors"
            case Compiler.Result.Crash(trace) => t"crashed: ${trace.show}"
        ```

        A process is also the sink the compilers write into — the three `#put` overloads — which
        is how a tool wrapping another compiler feeds one. Its `#version` is the version of the
        compiler which ran it, so a completed compilation can say what produced its output
        without the caller keeping the compiler in hand.
    refer anthology.Notice
    refer anthology.Compiler.Result
    refer anthology.Compiler.Progress
    entry .Update   one element of the live feed: a diagnostic, or a progress tick
        `Noticed(notice)` carries a `Notice` as the compiler reports it and `Progressed(progress)`
        a `Progress`, in the order they arrived; `#updates` yields them. A consumer
        matches on the two cases:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        def describe(update: Compiler.Process.Update): Text = update match
          case Compiler.Process.Update.Noticed(notice)      => notice.message
          case Compiler.Process.Update.Progressed(progress) => progress.stage
        ```
    entry #version   the version of the compiler which ran this compilation
    entry #put   records a notice, a progress tick, the result, or the task running the compilation
        Four overloads, for the compilers which feed a process: `put(notice)` appends a
        `Notice`, counts it as an error or a warning by its importance, and offers it to the
        feed; `put(progress)` offers a `Progress` to the feed; `put(result)` completes
        the process with a `Result`; and `put(task)` registers the `Task[Unit]` the
        compilation runs in, so that `#complete()` can wait for it to finish after the result.
    entry #errors   the number of error-level notices reported so far
    entry #warnings   the number of warning-level notices reported so far
    entry #complete   awaits the compilation's result, then stops the update feed
        Waits for the `Result` — `Success`, `Failure` or `Crash` — then waits for the
        compilation's task to finish and stops the feed, so that a consumer of `#updates` sees
        its end. It needs a `Monitor`, a `Tactic[Async.Error]` (for the await) and a
        `Event is Loggable`. After it returns, `#notices` is complete.
    entry #abort   asks the compiler to stop
        Clears an atomic flag the compiler polls through its progress callback, so an aborted
        compilation stops at its next progress tick rather than running to completion. The
        result it then completes with is whatever the compiler reports. `#cancelled` reads the
        flag back.
    entry #cancelled   whether `#abort()` has been called
    entry #updates   the live feed of diagnostics and progress ticks, as a stream of windows
        A single-owner stream over the process's relay: each window is an `Array` of
        `Process.Update`s, the first arriving when the first update does and the rest
        draining whatever else has arrived since. Claim it once, and consume it element-wise
        with `updates.records.each`. It ends when the compilation completes (`#complete()`
        stops the relay); a claim after completion replays the whole feed and then ends, which
        is how a finished process can still be inspected. It needs a `Buffering` in scope.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import logging.silentLogging
        import probates.cancelProbate
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import systems.javaBaseSystem
        import threads.virtualThreads

        val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
        val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]

        supervise:
          val process = Scalac[3.8](Nil)(classpath)(Map(t"a.scala" -> t"class A"), out)
          process.complete()

          process.updates.records.each:
            case Compiler.Process.Update.Progressed(progress) => println(progress.stage)
            case Compiler.Process.Update.Noticed(notice)      => println(notice.message)
        ```
    entry #notices   the diagnostics reported so far, oldest first
        A strict snapshot for post-hoc inspection, complete once `#complete()` has returned. A
        live consumer should prefer `#updates`, which also carries progress.
  entry .Result   how a compilation ended: success, failure, or a crash with its stack trace
      `Success` means the compiler ran and reported no errors; `Failure` that it ran and
      reported at least one, which the `Process`'s `notices` describe; and
      `Crash(error)` that the compiler itself threw, with the `StackTrace` it threw. A crash
      arrives as a value rather than an exception, and after one the stateless compilers
      replace their shared compiler instance (`Scalac.refresh()`, `Javac.refresh()`) and a
      `Scalac.Session` rebuilds its warm state, so the next compile starts clean.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def succeeded(result: Compiler.Result): Boolean = result match
        case Compiler.Result.Success  => true
        case Compiler.Result.Failure  => false
        case Compiler.Result.Crash(_) => false
      ```
  entry .Event   what a compiler logs as it runs: start, arguments, each diagnostic, a crash
    detail
        Every `Compiler.Event` carries the `Log.Compiler` tag. `Running(arguments)` is logged at
        `info` with the compiler's full argument list before it starts; `Notice(diagnostic)` at
        `fine` for each diagnostic as it is reported; `CompilerCrash` at `fail` when the
        compiler throws; and `Start` at `info` by `Javac` and `Kotlinc` before they run. A
        `Compiler.Event is Loggable` must be in scope for every compile; `logging.silentLogging`
        discards them, and `Event.relay` forwards them onto a `LinkEvent` channel.
    entry .communicable   renders each event as a message
        _starting compilation_, _the compiler crashed_, _the compiler emitted a diagnostic:
        `diagnostic`_ and _running the compiler with arguments `arguments`_.
    entry .relay   an `Event is Loggable` which forwards each event as a `LinkEvent.Message`
        The compile edges of a `Toolchain` are given the toolchain's `LinkEvent is Loggable`,
        while the compilers they drive speak `Event`; `Event.relay(using linkEvents)` bridges
        the two for the duration of a compile, so a build watching one path's events sees
        compilation and linking alike. Each event is rendered through `Event.communicable` and
        logged at the same level as a `LinkEvent.Message`. It captures whatever the link
        channel captures.
  entry .Progress   a progress tick: the fraction complete and the compiler's current stage
    detail
        `Scalac` reports one tick per whole percentage point, with the phase name the compiler
        is in (`typer`, `erasure`, …) as its `#stage`; `Javac` and `Kotlinc`, which expose no
        progress, report `0.1` on starting and `1.0` on success, with the compiler's name as the
        stage. Ticks arrive through `Process#updates` as `Update.Progressed`.
    entry #complete   the fraction of the compilation done, from `0.0` to `1.0`
    entry #stage   the compiler's current phase, as it names it

entry .Compilation   a compilation's output directory and classpath, tagged with its `Universe`
  detail
      A token pairing the directory a compilation wrote to with the `LocalClasspath` it was
      compiled against, and carrying the universe it inhabits as a type parameter, so that a
      `Compilation[Universe.Sjsir]` cannot be passed where a `Compilation[Universe.Nir]` is
      wanted: the two are different types, and the mismatch is a compile error in the build
      tool rather than a failure at link time.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val compilation: Compilation[Universe.Sjsir] = ???
      val native: Compilation[Universe.Nir] = compilation   // does not compile
      ```

      The `Toolchain` machinery flows the same pair untagged, as a `Deliverable.Emission`,
      since there the universe is a node of the graph rather than a type.
  entry #out   the directory the compilation wrote its output to
  entry #classpath   the classpath the sources were compiled against

entry .Notice   one diagnostic: its importance, file, message, span and semantic markup
  detail
      What a compiler reports about the sources, as a value: `#importance` is `Info`,
      `Warning` or `Error`; `#file` the name the source was given in the compile's `Map`
      (`hello.scala`), not a path on disk; `#message` the text; and `#span` the region of the
      source it concerns, as a denominative `Span`, or `Unset` for a diagnostic with no
      position. A tool can underline the offending code rather than echo the compiler's
      output.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def render(notice: Notice): Text = notice.span.lay(t"${notice.file}: ${notice.message}"): span =>
        t"${notice.file}:${span.start.line}: ${notice.message}"
      ```

      `#markup` is only set by `Scalac` under `scalacOptions.semanticDiagnostics`: the raw
      message with the compiler's in-band semantic markers (U+E000 and its companions)
      preserved, which the delicious library parses into a `SemanticMessage` of styled spans,
      placeholders and pickled types, so that a "found `A`, required `B`" message arrives as
      two types to render and compare rather than as text to re-parse.
  entry #importance   whether the notice is informational, a warning or an error
  entry #file   the name the source was given when it was compiled
  entry #message   the diagnostic's text, with any semantic markers stripped
  entry #span   the region of the source the notice concerns, or `Unset`
  entry #markup   the raw message with semantic markers, under `-Zsemantic-diagnostics`, else `Unset`

entry .Importance   the level of a diagnostic: `Info`, `Warning` or `Error`
    Ordered as the Scala compiler's own levels (its ordinal is the compiler's), so a notice
    counts as an error when its importance is `Importance.Error`. `Link.Error.Reason
    .CompilationFailed` counts its errors the same way:
    `notices.filter(_.importance == Importance.Error).length`.

entry .Language   the source languages, as the source nodes of a toolchain: `Scala`, `Java`, `Kotlin`
    An `Artifact.Source` per language the library has a compiler for, with ids `scala`,
    `java` and `kotlin`: the node a `Deliverable.Sources` sits at, from which `scalacEdges`,
    `javacEdges` and `kotlincEdges` lead to the universes each compiler can emit. It is
    **not exported** to the `soundness` umbrella, whose `Language` is cosmopolite's, so under
    `import soundness.*` it is written `anthology.Language.Scala`. `Artifact.source(t"scala")`
    is the same node.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val toolchain = Toolchain(List(scalacEdges.classfile(Scalac[3.8](Nil))), jarEdges())
    toolchain.path(anthology.Language.Scala, Artifact.Jar).map(_.tool.name)   // List(t"scalac", t"jar")
    ```

entry .NirPlugin   the location of the Scala Native compiler plugin, as evidence that NIR can be emitted
  detail
      NIR is emitted by a compiler plugin (`nscplugin`) rather than by a backend built into
      the Scala compiler, so compiling into `Universe.Nir` requires knowing where the plugin's
      JAR is. A `NirPlugin` given in scope is that evidence: `Universe.nir` builds the
      `Universe.Emission[Universe.Nir]` from it, adding `-Xplugin:<jar>` to the compiler's
      arguments, and `scalacEdges.nir` demands it. Without one, `Scalac#targeting[Universe.Nir]`
      cannot be applied and the edge cannot be built.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      given NirPlugin = NirPlugin(t"/home/user/.cache/nscplugin_3-0.5.12.jar".as[Path on Linux])
      summon[Universe.Emission[Universe.Nir]].flags   // List(t"-Xplugin:/home/user/.cache/nscplugin_3-0.5.12.jar")
      ```

      The stock published plugin (`org.scala-native:nscplugin_<scala version>:0.5.12`) works
      with the Soundness compiler; the Scala Native runtime (`scalalib`, `scala3lib`,
      `javalib`, `auxlib`, `clib`, `posixlib` and `nativelib`) goes on the classpath like any
      other library, and its absence shows as ordinary compiler diagnostics.
  entry #jar   the plugin JAR's path

entry .Artifact   a node of a toolchain: a source language, an intermediate representation or an application format
  detail
      Every node of a `Toolchain` is an `Artifact`, in one of three tiers: a `.Source` such as
      `anthology.Language.Scala`, which compilers consume; an `.Ir` such as a `Universe`,
      holding open-world, pre-link content in which libraries compose; or an `.Application`
      such as `Artifact.Jar` or `Apk`, a closed format a host runs. Tools consume any format
      but only ever produce an `Ir` or an `Application` — `Edge` says so in its types.

      A format's identity is its `#kind` and its `#id`, and nothing else: `equals` and
      `hashCode` are final and compare only those. So wherever a parameter changes what a
      user receives — a JavaScript artifact's module system, a WASI artifact's interface
      version, a native binary's target triple — it is part of the `id`, and each
      parameterization is a distinct node. `Artifact.Binary(Triple.Arm64MacOs)` is as
      distinct from `Artifact.Binary(Triple.X64Linux)` as a JAR is from a JavaScript bundle;
      that they share a linker does not make them interchangeable.

      The node set is open. A format can be declared as data, by its kind and name, with
      `.apply`, `.source`, `.ir` and `.application`, as a registry read at run time declares
      the forms no Soundness release knows; and a declared format is the *same node* as the
      built-in format of its kind and name, so `Artifact.ir(t"classfile")` reaches every edge
      registered for `Universe.Classfile`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Artifact.ir(t"classfile") == Universe.Classfile                   // true
      Artifact(Artifact.Kind.Application, t"apk") == Apk                // true
      Artifact.source(t"wasm") == Artifact.application(t"wasm")         // false: different kinds
      Artifact.Binary(Triple.Arm64MacOs) == Artifact.Binary(Triple.X64Linux)   // false
      ```

      A class extending `Artifact` other than through one of the three tier traits must
      define `#kind` itself, and none may override `equals` or `hashCode`; a case class
      extending it has no synthesized structural equality, so a format whose parameters
      distinguish nodes must encode them in its `id`, as every built-in format does.
  refer anthology.Universe
  refer anthology.Toolchain
  refer anthology.Edge
  entry .Kind   the tier a format belongs to: `Source`, `Ir` or `Application`
      The kind a format is declared with, as data. Two formats are equal only when their
      kinds are, so a source and an application may share a name.
  entry .Source   the tier of formats compilers consume: a source language
      `kind` is fixed to `Kind.Source`. The built-in sources are the cases of
      `anthology.Language`; `Artifact.source(id)` declares another.
  entry .Ir   the tier of intermediate representations: classfiles, `.sjsir`, `.nir`, components
      `kind` is fixed to `Kind.Ir`. The built-in intermediate representations are the
      `Universe`s a Scala compilation emits, plus the edgeless `Component` and `WasmObject`;
      `Artifact.ir(id)` declares another.
  entry .Application   the tier of closed formats a host runs: a JAR, an APK, a bundle, a binary
      `kind` is fixed to `Kind.Application`. The built-in applications are `Artifact.Jar`,
      `Artifact.Binary`, `Library`, `anthology.Js`, `anthology.Wasm`, `Wasi`, `OciImage`,
      `Dex` and `Apk`; `Artifact.application(id)` declares another.
  entry .Jar   an executable JAR, bound to the JDK: the node `jarEdges` reaches from `Classfile`
      The application node with id `jar`: an executable JAR of a compilation's output together
      with its whole classpath, with the single `EntryPoint` as its `Main-Class`. It is
      nested here as `Artifact.Jar` because the `soundness` umbrella's `Jar` is zeppelin's.
  entry .Binary   a machine-code executable for one target `Triple`: one node per triple
    detail
        The application node with id `binary-<triple>`, `binary-arm64-apple-darwin` for
        example, reached from `Universe.Nir` through `nativeEdges`. A binary is bound to one
        operating system and architecture's C library, so each triple is its own node and a
        toolchain built with `nativeEdges(Triple.X64Linux)` has no path to
        `Artifact.Binary(Triple.Arm64MacOs)`. Nested here as `Artifact.Binary` because the
        umbrella's `Binary` is monotonous's.
    entry #triple   the target platform the binary runs on
  entry .apply   `Artifact(kind, id)`: a format declared as data, by its kind and name
      Dispatches on the kind to `.source`, `.ir` or `.application`. The result is equal to,
      and hashes as, the built-in format of that kind and id, if one exists.
  entry .source   `Artifact.source(id)`: a declared source format
      `Artifact.source(t"proto")`, for a language no Soundness compiler knows but a registered
      edge can consume. It inspects as `Artifact.source(proto)`.
  entry .ir   `Artifact.ir(id)`: a declared intermediate representation
      `Artifact.ir(t"descriptor")`, a universe with its own carrier. `Artifact.ir(t"classfile")`
      is `Universe.Classfile`.
  entry .application   `Artifact.application(id)`: a declared application format
      `Artifact.application(t"bundle")`, a deliverable with its own host contract.
      `Artifact.application(t"js-es")` is `anthology.Js(anthology.Js.Module.Es)`.
  entry #id   the format's name, which with its kind is its whole identity
  entry #kind   the tier the format belongs to

entry .Universe   the intermediate representation a compilation emits: `Classfile`, `Sjsir` or `Nir`
  detail
      A compilation emits one intermediate representation, and that choice decides which
      library artifacts it can link against and which application formats it can become.
      `Classfile` is JVM classfiles, linkable as an `Artifact.Jar`, a `Dex` or an `Apk`;
      `Sjsir` is Scala.js IR, whose linked form — JavaScript, browser Wasm or a WASI component —
      is chosen at link time; `Nir` is Scala Native IR, linked to machine code through LLVM.
      Each universe is an `Artifact.Ir` node of a `Toolchain`, with ids `classfile`, `sjsir`
      and `nir`, and every universe additionally has a `Library` JAR packaging its unlinked
      output.

      The universe is also a *type*, the second type parameter of `Scalac`, so that a
      compiler configured for one cannot be used as another: `Scalac[3.8](options)` targets
      `Universe.Classfile`, and `targeting[Universe.Sjsir]` retargets it. The compiler flags
      each universe needs follow from the `.Emission` given for it — none for classfiles,
      `-scalajs` for Scala.js, and the plugin flag for Native, which exists only when a
      `NirPlugin` is in scope — rather than being supplied by hand.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val forJs: Scalac[3.8, Universe.Sjsir] = Scalac[3.8](Nil).targeting[Universe.Sjsir]
      summon[Universe.Emission[Universe.Sjsir]].flags   // List(t"-scalajs")
      ```

      Each universe beyond the JVM's has runtime prerequisites, supplied on the classpath like
      any other library: `Sjsir` needs `scalajs-javalib`, `scalajs-library_2.13`,
      `scalajs-scalalib_2.13` and `scala3-library_sjs1`, and `Nir` the Scala Native runtime
      (`scalalib`, `scala3lib`, `javalib`, `auxlib`, `clib`, `posixlib`, `nativelib`). Their
      absence surfaces as ordinary compiler diagnostics rather than as a distinct failure.
  refer anthology.Scalac
  refer anthology.Compilation
  refer anthology.NirPlugin
  entry .Classfile   JVM classfiles; also the type `Universe.Classfile`, the case's singleton type
      The universe a plain `Scalac[version](options)` targets, and the only one `Javac` and
      `Kotlinc` emit. It links as an `Artifact.Jar`, a `Library(Universe.Classfile)`, a `Dex`
      or an `Apk`. The type alias `Universe.Classfile` is `Universe.Classfile.type`, so the
      case can be named as a type parameter: `Compilation[Universe.Classfile]`.
  entry .Sjsir   Scala.js IR; also the type `Universe.Sjsir`
      Reached with `scalac.targeting[Universe.Sjsir]`, which adds `-scalajs`. It links through
      `sjsEdges` as JavaScript under any module system, as browser Wasm, or — given a
      `WasiToolchain` and a `Wasi.World` — as a WASI component, and packages as
      `Library(Universe.Sjsir)`.
  entry .Nir   Scala Native IR; also the type `Universe.Nir`
      Reached with `scalac.targeting[Universe.Nir]`, which needs a `NirPlugin` given for its
      `-Xplugin:` flag. It links through `nativeEdges` to an `Artifact.Binary` per target
      triple, and packages as `Library(Universe.Nir)`.
  entry .Emission   the compiler flags a universe's emission needs, as a typeclass over the universe
    detail
        `Universe.Emission[universe]` is what `Scalac#apply` and `Scalac.Setup`'s session
        summon to learn the flags to add for the universe the compiler targets. The three
        givens `.classfile`, `.sjsir` and `.nir` live in this companion, so no import is
        needed; the last exists only when a `NirPlugin` is in scope, which is what makes
        compiling for Native impossible without naming the plugin.
    entry #flags   the arguments to add to the compiler's command line
  entry .classfile   the `Emission[Classfile]` given: no extra flags
  entry .sjsir   the `Emission[Sjsir]` given: `-scalajs`
  entry .nir   the `Emission[Nir]` given, built from the `NirPlugin` in scope: `-Xplugin:<jar>`
      Summoning it without a `NirPlugin` given is a compile error, so a `Scalac` retargeted to
      `Universe.Nir` cannot be applied until the plugin's location is in scope.
  entry #section   the label of the LIRA section holding this universe's content: `jvm`, `sjsir` or `nir`
  entry #suffixes   the filename suffixes of this universe's stored forms, with `.tasty`
      `.class` and `.tasty` for `Classfile`, `.sjsir` and `.tasty` for `Sjsir`, `.nir` and
      `.tasty` for `Nir`: each universe's binary form, plus the TASTy which carries its
      interface.

entry .Deliverable   the value flowing along a toolchain path: sources, an emission, or a product
  detail
      What each edge's tool consumes and produces, and its case says where in the graph it
      belongs: `Sources(sources, classpath)` — a map of named sources and the classpath to
      compile them against — sits at a source-language node; `Emission(out, classpath)` — a
      compilation's output directory and the classpath it was compiled against — at an
      intermediate representation; and `Product(file)` — a linked or packaged file — at an
      application. `Toolchain#produce` takes one as its input, at the node it names as its
      source.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]

      val fromSource = Deliverable.Sources(Map(t"hello.scala" -> t"object Main"), classpath)
      val fromClasses = Deliverable.Emission(out, classpath)
      val fromJar = Deliverable.Product(out / "app.jar")
      ```

      A tool knows statically which variant its edge consumes, and the three accessors
      `#sources`, `#emission` and `#product` are its coercions: each raises `Link.Error` with
      reason `UnexpectedInput(format)` when given another variant, naming the format under
      production, which is how a miswired toolchain is diagnosed.
  refer anthology.Toolchain
  refer anthology.Tool
  entry #sources   the named sources and classpath, for a compile edge
      Raises `Link.Error(UnexpectedInput(target.id))` unless this is a `Sources`.
  entry #emission   the output directory and classpath, for a link or packaging edge
      Raises `Link.Error(UnexpectedInput(target.id))` unless this is an `Emission`.
  entry #product   the file, for an edge which repackages a finished product
      The `Apk` and `OciImage` tools consume the product of the edge before them this way.
      Raises `Link.Error(UnexpectedInput(target.id))` unless this is a `Product`.

entry .Edge   one edge of a toolchain: a tool producing the `target` format from the `source` format
  detail
      `Edge(source, target, tool)`. The target is typed `Artifact.Ir | Artifact.Application`,
      since sources are never produced. The edge providers — `jarEdges()`, `dexEdges()`,
      `apkEdges()`, `sjsEdges()`, `nativeEdges()`, `ociEdges()`, `scalacEdges.classfile(…)`,
      `javacEdges(…)`, `kotlincEdges(…)` — each return the edges for their tool, and
      `Toolchain(…)` assembles any number of such lists; an edge for a new format is written
      directly, with a `Tool` of its own.

      ```scala fuel
      substitute ???  ...
      ##
      import soundness.*

      val proto = Artifact.source(t"proto")
      val descriptor = Artifact.ir(t"descriptor")

      def protoc: Tool = ???
      val edge = Edge(proto, descriptor, protoc)
      ```
  entry #source   the format the tool consumes
  entry #target   the format the tool produces: an intermediate representation or an application
  entry #tool   the tool which runs the edge

entry .Tool   a toolchain edge's implementation: a compiler, linker or packager, with its settings type
  detail
      A `Tool` is an inert value: the capabilities its execution needs — a `Monitor`, a
      `System`, a `WorkingDirectory` — flow through `#run`'s context rather than being
      captured when it is built. It declares a `#Settings` type and an `#initial`
      configuration of that type, which `Toolchain#produce` folds the applicable
      `Toolchain.Setting`s into before calling `#run`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def passThrough(label: Text): Tool = new Tool:
        type Settings = Unit

        def name: Text = label
        def initial: Unit = ()

        def run
          ( settings:    Unit,
            input:       Deliverable,
            entryPoints: List[EntryPoint],
            out:         Path on Linux )
          ( using Monitor, System, WorkingDirectory )
          ( using Tactic[Link.Error], (LinkEvent is Loggable)^ )
        :   Deliverable =

          input
      ```

      The built-in tools are private to their edge providers; what a user holds is the
      `Edge`, whose `tool.name` is what a path reports.
  refer anthology.Edge
  refer anthology.Toolchain
  entry #Settings   the configuration type this tool is driven by
      `Unit` for the compile edges (the compiler carries its own options), `Text` for the JAR
      edges (the filename), the Scala.js `StandardConfig` for `sjsEdges`, Scala Native's
      `NativeConfig` for `nativeEdges`, `Apk.Configuration` for `apkEdges` and
      `OciConfiguration` for `ociEdges`.
  entry #name   the tool's name, as a path reports it: `scalac`, `jar`, `dex`, `apk`, `oci`, …
  entry #initial   the default settings, before any `Toolchain.Setting` is applied
  entry #run   runs the tool on a deliverable, writing into a directory, and returns what it produced
      `run(settings, input, entryPoints, out)`: `input` is the `Deliverable` at the edge's
      source node, `entryPoints` are ambient along the whole path (each tool applies or
      ignores them as its format demands), and `out` is the directory to write into. It needs
      a `Monitor`, `System` and `WorkingDirectory`, raises `Link.Error`, and logs
      `LinkEvent`s.

entry .Toolchain   a graph of formats and tools, over which producing one format from another is path search
  detail
      A `Toolchain` is a directed acyclic graph whose nodes are `Artifact`s and whose edges
      are `Tool`s. `#produce` takes what you have, the format you want and an output
      location, finds the unique shortest path between the two nodes, and runs each tool on
      it in turn; `#path` reports the route without running anything. The graph is assembled
      from edge lists with `.apply`, which rejects a duplicate edge between the same pair of
      formats and rejects a cycle.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label An executable JAR links straight from classfiles
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val toolchain = Toolchain(jarEdges())
            toolchain.path(Universe.Classfile, Artifact.Jar).map(_.tool.name)   // List(t"jar")
      frame
        label Adding the dex edges makes `Dex` reachable, one step away
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val toolchain = Toolchain(jarEdges(), dexEdges())
            toolchain.path(Universe.Classfile, Dex).map(_.target.id)   // List(t"dex")
      frame
        label Adding the APK edge extends the path: classfiles are dexed, then packaged
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val toolchain = Toolchain(jarEdges(), dexEdges(), apkEdges())
            toolchain.path(Universe.Classfile, Apk).map(_.target.id)   // List(t"dex", t"apk")
      ##
      ```

      Nothing about the two-stage sequence is written into the APK tool: the path composes
      it. A format which cannot be reached raises `Link.Error` with reason `NoPath`, naming
      the two formats, rather than failing part-way through; two equally short routes raise
      `AmbiguousPath`, resolved by producing an intermediate format explicitly in a separate
      step rather than by a tie-break the caller cannot see.

      Tools are configured by `.Setting`s addressed to the *formats* whose production they
      configure, not to tools: `apkOptions.minApi(24)` applies to both `Dex` and `Apk`, since
      the API level governs both how D8 desugars and what the manifest declares, and
      `linkerOptions.optimize.fast` to every format the Scala.js linker produces. A setting
      matching no format on the path is rejected before any tool runs.
  refer anthology.Edge
  refer anthology.Deliverable
  refer anthology.Link
  entry .apply   assembles a toolchain from any number of edge lists, checking for duplicates and cycles
      `Toolchain(jarEdges(), dexEdges(), apkEdges())`, or `Toolchain(List(edge1, edge2))`.
      Raises `Link.Error` with reason `DuplicateEdge(source, target)` when two edges join the
      same pair of formats, and `CyclicToolchain` when the edges form a cycle; a toolchain
      which exists is therefore a DAG whose every pair of nodes has at most one direct edge.
  entry .Error   raised when a native tool a toolchain shells out to is not on the `PATH`
    detail
        Error 586: _the native tool `tool` is not available on the PATH_. Raised by the
        probing constructors — `WasiToolchain()` for `wasm-tools` and `wit-bindgen`,
        `nativeEdges()` for `clang` and `clang++` — so that an edge whose tooling is absent
        cannot be built, and a missing tool is found when the toolchain is assembled, not
        when a link fails. `nativeEdges()` also reports an unrecognised build host, which
        cannot name its own triple, as the absence of `clang`.
    entry #tool   the name of the missing tool
  entry .Setting   a tool setting, addressed to the formats whose production it configures
    detail
        A `Toolchain` applies each setting, in the order given, to the initial settings of
        every edge on the path whose target `#appliesTo` accepts; `#edit` then transforms
        that edge's settings. The contract is that `appliesTo(format)` implies `edit(format,
        _)` accepts and returns the settings type of every edge targeting `format`; a setting
        spanning formats with different settings types — the Android API level configures
        both dexing and packaging — dispatches on its `format` argument.

        The option objects (`jarOptions`, `linkerOptions`, `nativeOptions`, `dexOptions`,
        `apkOptions`, `ociOptions`) are the built-in settings; `.apply` makes one for a tool
        of the caller's own.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val everyJar: Toolchain.Setting =
          Toolchain.Setting[Text](format => format == Artifact.Jar)(_ => t"renamed.jar")
        ```
    entry .apply   `Toolchain.Setting[settings](appliesTo)(edit)`: a setting over one settings type
        The first argument says which formats the setting configures; the second edits the
        settings of every edge targeting one of them, typed as `settings`. An `edit` which
        throws fails the production with `Link.Error` reason `Failed`.
    entry #appliesTo   whether the setting configures the production of a format
    entry #edit   the setting applied to an edge's settings, given the format that edge produces
  entry #edges   the toolchain's edges
  entry #path   the unique shortest path between two formats, as the edges to run in order
      `toolchain.path(Universe.Classfile, Apk)` is the list of `Edge`s from the first to the
      second; a format is a zero-length path from itself. Raises `Link.Error` with reason
      `NoPath(source, target)` when no route exists — against the direction of the edges, or
      to a node whose edge was never registered — and `AmbiguousPath(source, target)` when two
      or more routes are equally short. A declared format (`Artifact.ir(t"classfile")`) routes
      exactly as the built-in it equals.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val android = Toolchain(jarEdges(), dexEdges(), apkEdges())
      android.path(Dex, Dex)                                    // Nil
      android.path(Universe.Classfile, Apk).map(_.tool.name)    // List(t"dex", t"apk")
      safely(android.path(Apk, Universe.Classfile))             // Unset: no path backwards
      ```
  entry #produce   produces the target format from the input by running each tool on the path, and returns the output's path
      `produce(input, source, target, out, settings = Nil, entryPoints = Nil)`: `input` is the
      `Deliverable` at `source`; each intermediate product on a multi-stage path is written
      below `out` in a directory named by its format's `id` (an APK's dex archive lands in
      `out/dex/`), and the final product in `out` itself. The result is the final `Product`'s
      file, or, when the target is an intermediate representation such as
      `Universe.Classfile`, the `Emission`'s directory.

      Every setting is checked against the path's targets first, and one which applies to
      none raises `Link.Error` with reason `InapplicableSetting` before any tool runs. Entry
      points are ambient along the whole path: an executable JAR takes at most one as its
      `Main-Class`, a native binary and an APK require exactly one, and a `Library` or `Dex`
      ignores them.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]
      val staged: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/staged".as[Path on Linux]

      supervise:
        Toolchain(jarEdges(), dexEdges(), apkEdges()).produce
          ( Deliverable.Emission(out, classpath),
            Universe.Classfile,
            Apk,
            staged,
            List(apkOptions.minApi(26), apkOptions.packageName(t"com.example.app")),
            List(EntryPoint(Fqcn(t"com.example.MainActivity"))) )
      ```

      It needs a `Monitor`, a `System` and a `WorkingDirectory`, raises `Link.Error`, and logs
      `LinkEvent.Start` before the first tool and `LinkEvent.Linked` with the output's path
      after the last; a compile edge on the path relays its `Compiler.Event`s onto the same
      channel.

entry .Component   the WebAssembly component universe: an intermediate node with no edges yet
    An `Artifact.Ir` with id `component`: WebAssembly library components, composing with one
    another through interfaces described by WIT, as TASTy lets `sjsir` and `nir` compose. It
    is an `Ir` rather than a `Universe`, since no Scala compilation emits it — a component is
    an application (`Wasi`) when the world it exports is runnable, and content in this
    universe when it is composed with others. No built-in edge produces or consumes it; the
    node exists so that composition tools can register theirs, and a path to it raises
    `NoPath` until one does.

entry .EntryPoint   a class whose `main(args: Array[String])` runs when the linked artifact loads
  detail
      `EntryPoint(Fqcn(t"com.example.Main"))`. Entry points are passed to `Toolchain#produce`
      and are ambient along the whole path, each tool applying or ignoring them as its
      format demands: an executable `Artifact.Jar` takes at most one, as its `Main-Class`
      (none means a JAR with no main class; two raise `Link.Error` reason `ManyEntryPoints`);
      a native `Artifact.Binary` requires exactly one (`NoEntryPoint` otherwise); an `Apk`
      requires exactly one, which is its launcher activity; JavaScript and Wasm links
      initialise each as a module initializer; and a `Library` or a `Dex` ignores them
      entirely, since neither records an entry point.
  entry #mainClass   the fully-qualified class name

entry .Link   the namespace of `Link.Error`, the failure of linking, path search or packaging
  detail
      Everything a `Toolchain` can fail with is a `Link.Error` carrying a `Link.Error.Reason`,
      so one `Tactic[Link.Error]` covers assembling a toolchain, searching it and running its
      tools. A tool's own fault (a thrown exception) is `Failed` with its stack trace; a
      structural fault (no path, a duplicate edge, a setting with nothing to configure) is
      its own reason; and a compile edge's failure is `CompilationFailed`, carrying every
      `Notice` the compiler reported.
  entry .Error   raised by a toolchain: error 443, _linking failed because `reason`_
    detail
        `Link.Error(reason)`; its number's detail part is the reason's own number. Match on
        `#reason` to tell the cases apart:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        def explain(error: Link.Error): Text = error.reason match
          case Link.Error.Reason.NoPath(source, target)         => t"no route from $source to $target"
          case Link.Error.Reason.CompilationFailed(notices)     => t"${notices.length} diagnostics"
          case Link.Error.Reason.Failed(trace)                  => trace.show
          case other                                            => other.communicate.text
        ```
    entry .Reason   why linking failed: fourteen numbered cases, each with its message
      detail
          Each case carries its number as `#number`, which is the detail part of the error's
          code (`443.5` for `NoPath`):

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `Failed(trace)`  1  _the linker terminated abnormally_  a tool threw; the `StackTrace` is kept
          row  `NoEntryPoint`  3  _a native executable requires exactly one entry point_  also raised by the APK tool, which needs its launcher activity
          row  `ManyEntryPoints`  4  _an executable JAR permits at most one entry point_
          row  `NoPath(source, target)`  5  _the toolchain has no path from `source` to `target`_
          row  `AmbiguousPath(source, target)`  6  _the toolchain has several shortest paths from `source` to `target`, so an intermediate format must be produced explicitly_
          row  `InapplicableSetting`  7  _a setting applies to no format produced on the path_
          row  `DuplicateEdge(source, target)`  8  _the toolchain declares more than one edge from `source` to `target`_
          row  `CyclicToolchain`  9  _the toolchain's edges form a cycle_
          row  `UnexpectedInput(format)`  10  _the tool producing `format` cannot consume the content it was given_  a `Deliverable` of the wrong variant reached a tool
          row  `CompilationFailed(notices)`  11  _compilation failed with N errors_  N counts the notices whose importance is `Error`; the list holds every diagnostic
          row  `MissingSetting(name)`  12  _the setting `name` is required but unspecified_
          row  `Packaging(detail)`  13  _packaging failed: `detail`_  a zip, path, I/O or filename fault while bundling a JAR, dexing or packaging an APK, with the underlying error's message
          row  `CompilerCrash`  14  _the compiler crashed_  a compile edge's compiler threw
          row  `CompilerUnusable(detail)`  15  _the compiler could not be run: `detail`_  a compile edge could not configure or await its compiler (`scalac`, `javac`, `kotlinc`)
          ```
      entry #number   the reason's number, the detail part of the error code
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as the message listed under `Link.Error.Reason`
    entry #reason   why linking failed

entry .LinkEvent   what a toolchain logs as it links: start, each message, and the output's path
  detail
      Every `LinkEvent` carries the `Log.Compiler` tag. `Toolchain#produce` logs `Start` at
      `info` before the first tool and `Linked(path)` at `info` after the last; every tool
      relays its linker's own output as `Message(message)` — the Scala.js linker's errors and
      warnings at `warn`, its info at `info` and the rest at `fine`; D8's errors and warnings
      at `warn`; and, through `Compiler.Event.relay`, a compile edge's `Compiler.Event`s at their
      own levels. A `LinkEvent is Loggable` must be in scope for every `produce`.
  entry .communicable   renders each event as a message
      _starting linking_, _the linker reported: `message`_ and _linked output written to
      `path`_.

entry .Triple   a target platform for a native link, rendered as an LLVM target triple
  detail
      Six targets: `Arm64MacOs`, `X64MacOs`, `Arm64Linux`, `X64Linux`, `Arm64Windows` and
      `X64Windows`, each rendering through `#text` as the LLVM triple Scala Native is given
      (`arm64-apple-darwin`, `x86_64-apple-darwin`, `aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu`,
      `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`, `aarch64-pc-windows-msvc`, `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`).
      `Artifact.Binary(triple)` is the application node for a binary on that platform, and
      `nativeEdges(triples*)` registers one edge per triple. Targets beyond the build host
      require a C toolchain and sysroot capable of cross-compiling to them.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Triple.Arm64MacOs.text                       // t"arm64-apple-darwin"
      Triple.host.let(Artifact.Binary(_))          // the build host's own binary node, or Unset
      ```
  refer anthology.nativeEdges
  entry .host   the build host's own triple, from `os.name` and `os.arch`, or `Unset` if unrecognised
      macOS, Linux and Windows on ARM64 or x86-64 are recognised; any other platform is
      `Unset`, and `nativeEdges()` with no explicit triples reports it as the absence of
      `clang`, since such a host has no Scala Native runtime either.
  entry #text   the LLVM target triple

entry .WasmObject   the relocatable-WebAssembly-object universe: an intermediate node with no edges yet
    An `Artifact.Ir` with id `wasm-object`: relocatable WebAssembly objects composing by the
    symbols of their linking sections — what `rustc --target wasm32` and clang emit, linked
    by `wasm-ld` into a WASI 0.1 module or lifted by component tools into the `Component`
    universe. No Scala compilation emits it and no built-in edge produces or consumes it;
    the node exists so that foreign compilers and wasm linkers can register theirs.

entry .Scalac   the Scala compiler as a value: typed options, a language version and a target universe
  detail
      A `Scalac[version, universe]` carries a list of `Scalac.Option`s, each typed by the
      language versions which accept it, and the `Universe` it emits. `Scalac[3.8](options)`
      — the single-type-argument form — targets `Universe.Classfile`; `#targeting` retargets
      it. Applied to a classpath and sources, it compiles in-process, in a task of its own,
      and returns a `Compiler.Process`:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import logging.silentLogging
      import probates.cancelProbate
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]

      supervise:
        val compiler = Scalac[3.8](List(scalacOptions.newSyntax, scalacOptions.warnings.feature))
        val process = compiler(classpath)(Map(t"hello.scala" -> t"object Main"), out)
        process.complete()
      ```

      The version parameter is a *declared language target* which gates the options
      statically: an option whose versions do not include the compiler's is a compile error
      in the tool, not a complaint from the compiler at run time. It does not select a
      compiler. The compiler which actually runs is whichever `scala3-compiler` is on the
      program's runtime classpath — a compile-only dependency of `anthology.scala`, so the
      driving program chooses it — and `.version` reports which that is.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label An option valid at every version
        code
            import soundness.*

            val compiler = Scalac[3.8](List(scalacOptions.newSyntax))
            compiler.commandLineArguments   // List(t"-new-syntax")
      frame
        label `experimental` is accepted from 3.4 to 3.8, so 3.8 may take it
        code
            import soundness.*

            val compiler = Scalac[3.8](List(scalacOptions.newSyntax, scalacOptions.experimental))
            compiler.commandLineArguments   // List(t"-new-syntax", t"-experimental")
      frame
        label `semanticDiagnostics` exists only at 3.9: the 3.8 compiler rejects it
        code
            import soundness.*

            val compiler = Scalac[3.8](List(scalacOptions.newSyntax, scalacOptions.semanticDiagnostics))   // does not compile
      frame
        label Retargeting keeps the options and changes the universe
        code
            import soundness.*

            val compiler = Scalac[3.8](List(scalacOptions.newSyntax, scalacOptions.experimental))
            val forJs: Scalac[3.8, Universe.Sjsir] = compiler.targeting[Universe.Sjsir]
            forJs.commandLineArguments   // List(t"-new-syntax", t"-experimental"); `-scalajs` is added by the emission
      ##
      ```

      Besides the stateless `#apply`, a compiler bound to a classpath with `#on` opens a
      *warm session* — `scalac.on(classpath).session: …` — in which several compiles share one
      loaded symbol table, each seeing what the previous ones defined; see `Scalac.Session`.
      The compile edges of a `Toolchain` (`scalacEdges`) carry a `Scalac` the same way.

      The stateless compiles share one compiler instance across the program (`.compiler()`),
      replaced by `.refresh()` after a crash. Diagnostics become `Notice`s with their spans;
      under `scalacOptions.semanticDiagnostics` each also keeps its semantic markup. Progress
      is reported per whole percentage point, with the compiler's phase as the stage, and
      `Compiler.Process#abort()` is observed at the next tick.
  refer anthology.scalacOptions
  refer anthology.scalacEdges
  refer anthology.Universe
  refer anthology.Compiler.Process
  entry .Versions   the language versions a `Scalac` can be declared for: `3.0` to `3.9`
      The union of literal types `3.0 | 3.1 | … | 3.9`, the bound of a `Scalac`'s version
      parameter and the widest version an `Option` can be declared for. A version is a
      literal, `Scalac[3.8]`, never a value.
  entry .Option   a compiler option, as the flags it adds, typed by the versions which accept it
    detail
        `Scalac.Option[-version <: Versions](flags*)`: the version parameter is
        *contravariant*, so an option declared for `3.4 | 3.5 | 3.6` is accepted by a
        `Scalac[3.5]`, and one declared for `Scalac.Versions` by any. The `scalacOptions`
        object holds the built-in ones; an option the object lacks is written directly:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val color = Scalac.Option[Scalac.Versions](t"-color", t"never")
        Scalac[3.8](List(color)).commandLineArguments   // List(t"-color", t"never")
        ```
    entry #flags   the command-line arguments the option contributes, in order
  entry .refresh   replaces the shared compiler instance, as happens after a crash
      The stateless compiles share one `dotty.tools.dotc.Compiler`; a crash replaces it
      automatically, and `refresh()` does so on demand.
  entry .compiler   the shared compiler instance the stateless compiles run on
  entry .version   the version of the embedded compiler, as it reports itself
      `dotty.tools.dotc.config.Properties.versionNumberString`: the version of the
      `scala3-compiler` on the runtime classpath, which is what every `Scalac`'s `#version`
      and every process's `version` report.
  entry .apply   `Scalac[version](options)`: a compiler for the classfile universe
      The single-type-argument form, which fixes the universe to `Universe.Classfile`;
      `#targeting` changes it. Each option's versions must include the one named.
  entry .Setup   a compiler bound to a classpath: the subject of a warm session
    detail
        `scalac.on(classpath)` makes one, and `.session` on it opens a `Scalac.Session`
        through aperture's `Sessional` — the `.sessional` given here — in which the
        classpath's symbol table, loaded once, is retained across compiles. A `Setup` is only
        the pair; the session is where the state lives.
    entry .sessional   the `Sessional` given which opens a `Scalac.Session` from a `Setup`
        In the companion, so no import is needed. It needs a `System`, the
        `Universe.Emission` for the setup's universe, a `Tactic[Compiler.Error]` and a
        `Compiler.Event is Loggable`, and it is what `setup.session: …` resolves to.
    entry #scalac   the compiler, with its options and universe
    entry #classpath   the classpath the session compiles against
  entry .Session   a warm compiler session: one retained symbol table across several compiles
    detail
        Opened with `scalac.on(classpath).session: …`; inside the block the session is the
        contextual `Scalac.Session^`, reached as `compilation`, and `sources.compile()` or
        `compilation.compile(sources)` compiles within it. One base context, whose symbol
        table persists from run to run as in the REPL, and one compiler are reused, so the
        second compile sees the first's classes and pays for none of the classpath's loading
        again. Compiles run synchronously on the caller's thread (the compiler pins a
        context to the thread of its first run), and the `.Process` returned is complete by
        the time it is returned.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import systems.javaBaseSystem

        val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
        val alpha = Map(t"alpha.scala" -> t"class Alpha:\n  def x: Int = 42\n")
        val beta = Map(t"beta.scala" -> t"class Beta:\n  def alpha: Alpha = Alpha()\n")

        Scalac[3.8](Nil).on(classpath).session:
          alpha.compile().complete()
          val process = beta.compile()
          process.complete()                    // Compiler.Result.Success: `Alpha` is known
          process.classfiles.keys.to[List]      // the emitted classfiles, in memory
        ```

        Output is kept in memory: `Process#classfiles` exposes every emitted artifact keyed by
        its classpath path, and `Process#save(directory)` writes them to disk only if wanted.
        A failed compile leaves the session usable; a crash rebuilds its compiler and
        context together, so the next compile starts clean. Opening a session with options
        the compiler rejects raises `Compiler.Error`.

        Two limitations follow from the REPL-style retained symbol table: redefining a
        top-level name compiled earlier in the same session is unsupported (distinct source
        and class names per compile, as a REPL generates, are safe); and a macro defined by
        one compile loads at its next use from the *classpath*, not the retained symbols, so
        a caller wanting same-session macro expansion must `save` to a directory on the
        session's classpath. The session handle is a fresh, exclusive capability confined to
        the block, so it cannot be returned from it.
    refer anthology.compilation
    refer anthology.compile
    entry .Frontend   the session's compiler, base context, classpath and options, as they stand
      detail
          For a tool which types sources in the session without compiling them — harlequin's
          `highlighting.sessionScala` given takes one — and so sees what the session's
          compiles have defined, paying for none of the loading again. These are the
          compiler's own values: good only on the session's thread, and only until its next
          crash (which replaces them), so a caller takes `Session#frontend` afresh for each
          use rather than keeping one.
      entry #compiler   the session's `dotty.tools.dotc.Compiler`
      entry #context   the base context whose symbol table the session has loaded and added to
      entry #classpath   the classpath the session was opened with, as text
      entry #options   the compiler arguments the session was opened with
    entry .Process   the process of one compile within a session, with its output held in memory
      detail
          A `Compiler.Process` whose result is already complete when it is returned, since
          session compiles run synchronously; `complete()` still works and returns it.
          `#classfiles` holds what the compile emitted and `#save` materialises it.
      entry #classfiles   every artifact the compile emitted, keyed by its path on the classpath
          Classfiles, TASTy, `.sjsir` or `.nir`, as a `Map[Path on Classpath, Data]` keyed by
          the location each would have on a classpath rooted at the output (`/Alpha.class`,
          `/pkg/Name.tasty`). Empty if the compile failed before the back end ran.

          ```scala fuel
          typecheck
          ##
          import soundness.*
          import logging.silentLogging
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import systems.javaBaseSystem

          val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath

          Scalac[3.8](Nil).on(classpath).session:
            val process = Map(t"alpha.scala" -> t"class Alpha").compile()
            process.classfiles.defines(t"/Alpha.class".as[Path on Classpath])   // true
          ```
      entry #save   writes the emitted artifacts below a directory, as `pkg/Name.class`
          Takes any path type `Abstractable across Paths to Text`, creating the package
          directories as needed. Saving to a directory on the session's classpath is how a
          macro compiled in the session becomes expandable by its later compiles.
    entry #frontend   the session's compiler and context as they stand, for typing without compiling
    entry #compile   compiles named sources within the session, synchronously
        `session.compile(sources)` — or, with the session contextual, `sources.compile()` —
        runs the compiler on the caller's thread against the retained symbol table and
        returns a `Scalac.Session.Process` whose result is already complete. The process
        borrows the session, so the next compile waits for it to be consumed.
  entry .Sessional   the `aperture.Sessional` instance class which opens a `Scalac.Session` for a `Setup`
    detail
        The named class behind `Scalac.Setup.sessional`; a user reaches it only through
        `setup.session: …`. Its `#Self` is `Scalac.Setup[version, universe]` and its `#Result`
        the fresh `Scalac.Session^` handle each `session` call confines to its block.
    entry #Self   the subject: `Scalac.Setup[version, universe]`
    entry #Result   the handle a session block receives: a fresh `Scalac.Session^`
    entry #session   runs a block with a new session as its contextual handle
        Nothing OS-level is torn down when the block ends: the warm context is only memory,
        reclaimed with the handle.
  entry #options   the compiler's options, in order
  entry #commandLineArguments   the flags of every option, concatenated in order
      What the options contribute; the universe's emission flags, `-d`, `-classpath` and the
      sources are added at the call.
  entry #version   the version of the embedded compiler, `Scalac.version`
  entry #targeting   the same compiler retargeted to another universe: `scalac.targeting[Universe.Sjsir]`
      Options are preserved; only the universe changes, and with it the emission flags the
      compile adds (`-scalajs`, or `-Xplugin:` for Native, which then needs a `NirPlugin`
      given at the call).
  entry #on   binds the compiler to a classpath, as a `Scalac.Setup`, ready to open a session
      `scalac.on(classpath).session: …`.
  entry #apply   compiles named sources against a classpath into a directory, in a task of its own
      `scalac(classpath)(sources, out)`: the compiler's arguments are the universe's emission
      flags, `-d out`, `-classpath`, then `#commandLineArguments`; it logs them as
      `Compiler.Event.Running`, starts a task named `scalac` which runs the compilation on the
      shared compiler, and returns the `Compiler.Process` at once. It needs a `System`, a
      `Monitor`, a `Probate`, the `Universe.Emission` for the compiler's universe, a
      `Tactic[Compiler.Error]` and a `Compiler.Event is Loggable`. The output directory must
      exist already: the compiler refuses a `-d` it cannot find, which a direct call does
      not create (the `scalacEdges` tools do). A crash becomes `Compiler.Result.Crash` and
      replaces the shared compiler.

      The same sources under the same compiler yield byte-identical output, run after run
      and whatever the output directory: the TASTy UUID is a hash of the file's own
      sections, and virtual sources carry the names they were given.

entry .scalacOptions   the Scala compiler's options as typed values, each valid only at the versions which accept it
  detail
      Mirrors the compiler's command line — syntax, warnings, language features, internal
      flags — as `Scalac.Option`s under `scalacOptions`, each typed by the language versions
      which accept it, so that `Scalac[3.8](List(scalacOptions.experimental))` compiles and
      `Scalac[3.9](List(scalacOptions.experimental))` does not. Nested objects group them:
      `warnings` (with `warnings.lint`), `internal`, `advanced` and
      `language.experimental`. The object is mirrored as a package of the `soundness`
      umbrella, so `scalacOptions.newSyntax` reads the same way from either.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val strict = Scalac[3.6]
        ( List
            ( scalacOptions.newSyntax,
              scalacOptions.warnings.deprecation,
              scalacOptions.warnings.unused(Unused.All),
              scalacOptions.internal.explicitNulls,
              scalacOptions.language.experimental.modularity ) )

      strict.commandLineArguments
      ```

      An option the object lacks is written directly as `Scalac.Option[Scalac.Versions](flags*)`.
  refer anthology.Scalac
  entry .newSyntax   `-new-syntax`, at every version
  entry .sourceFuture   `-source future`, at every version
  entry .experimental   `-experimental`, at 3.4 to 3.8
  entry .semanticDiagnostics   `-Zsemantic-diagnostics`, at 3.9: keeps each notice's semantic markup
      Under this option the compiler marks each interpolated argument of a message in band,
      with pickled TASTy for the types among them, and a `Notice` keeps that marked-up form
      as its `markup` alongside the plain `message`, for the delicious library to parse into
      a `SemanticMessage`.
  entry .warnings   the warning options: `-feature`, `-deprecation`, `-W…` flags and `-Wunused`
    detail
        Each is a `Scalac.Option` typed by the versions it exists at; `.unused` takes an
        `Unused` selection, and `.lint` holds the `-Wshadow` flags.
    entry .feature   `-feature`, at every version
    entry .deprecation   `-deprecation`, at every version
    entry .implausiblePatterns   `-Wimplausible-patterns`, at 3.3 to 3.6
    entry .enumCommentDiscard   `-Wenum-comment-discard`, at 3.4 to 3.6
    entry .unstableInlineAccessors   `-WunstableInlineAccessors`, at 3.4 to 3.6
    entry .nonUnitStatement   `-Wnonunit-statement`, at 3.4 to 3.6
    entry .valueDiscard   `-Wvalue-discard`, at 3.4 to 3.6
    entry .unused   `-Wunused:…` from an `Unused` selection: all, none, or a subset of features
        `warnings.unused(Unused.All)` is `-Wunused:all`, `Unused.None` is `-Wunused:none`, and
        `Unused.Subset(List(UnusedFeature.Privates, UnusedFeature.Locals))` is
        `-Wunused:privates,locals`. The option's version is the selection's.
    entry .lint   the `-Wshadow` lint flags
      detail
          `privateShadow` and `typeParameterShadow`, at 3.4 to 3.6.
      entry .privateShadow   `-Wshadow:private-shadow`
      entry .typeParameterShadow   `-Wshadow:type-parameter-shadow`
  entry .internal   the `-Y` flags: target names, safe init, explicit nulls, pattern checks, capture checking
    detail
        The compiler's internal options, as values.
    entry .requireTargetName   `-Yrequire-targetName`, at every version
    entry .safeInit   `-Ysafe-init`, at every version
    entry .explicitNulls   `-Yexplicit-nulls`, at every version
    entry .checkPatterns   `-Ycheck-all-patmat`, at every version
    entry .ccNew   `-Ycc-new`, at 3.4 to 3.6
    entry .ccDebug   `-Ycc-debug`, at 3.5 and 3.6
    entry .ccLog   `-Ycc-log`, at 3.5 and 3.6
  entry .advanced   the `-X` flags
    detail
        Only `maxInlines` so far; others are written directly as `Scalac.Option`s.
    entry .maxInlines   `-Xmax-inlines n`, at every version
  entry .language   the `-language:` options
    detail
        Holds `experimental`, the `-language:experimental.…` features.
    entry .experimental   the `-language:experimental.…` features, each at the versions which offer it
      detail
          One option per experimental language feature, named as the feature is:
          `clauseInterleaving` (3.3 to 3.6), `givenLoopPrevention` (3.4 to 3.6),
          `fewerBraces` (3.1 to 3.6), `into` (3.4 to 3.6), `relaxedExtensionImports` (3.3),
          `erasedDefinitions` (all), `saferExceptions` (3.2 to 3.6), `namedTypeArguments`
          (all), `pureFunctions` (3.3 to 3.6), `captureChecking` (3.3 to 3.6), `modularity`
          (3.5 and 3.6), `namedTuples` (3.5 and 3.6), `genericNumberLiterals` (all),
          `betterMatchTypeExtractors` (3.5 and 3.6), `quotedPatternsWithPolymorphicFunctions`
          (3.6) and `betterFors` (3.6).
      entry .clauseInterleaving   `-language:experimental.clauseInterleaving`, at 3.3 to 3.6
      entry .givenLoopPrevention   `-language:experimental.givenLoopPrevention`, at 3.4 to 3.6
      entry .fewerBraces   `-language:experimental.fewerBraces`, at 3.1 to 3.6
      entry .into   `-language:experimental.into`, at 3.4 to 3.6
      entry .relaxedExtensionImports   `-language:experimental.relaxedExtensionImports`, at 3.3
      entry .erasedDefinitions   `-language:experimental.erasedDefinitions`, at every version
      entry .saferExceptions   `-language:experimental.saferExceptions`, at 3.2 to 3.6
      entry .namedTypeArguments   `-language:experimental.namedTypeArguments`, at every version
      entry .pureFunctions   `-language:experimental.pureFunctions`, at 3.3 to 3.6
      entry .captureChecking   `-language:experimental.captureChecking`, at 3.3 to 3.6
      entry .modularity   `-language:experimental.modularity`, at 3.5 and 3.6
      entry .namedTuples   `-language:experimental.namedTuples`, at 3.5 and 3.6
      entry .genericNumberLiterals   `-language:experimental.genericNumberLiterals`, at every version
      entry .betterMatchTypeExtractors   `-language:experimental.betterMatchTypeExtractors`, at 3.5 and 3.6
      entry .quotedPatternsWithPolymorphicFunctions   `-language:experimental.quotedPatternsWithPolymorphicFunctions`, at 3.6
      entry .betterFors   `-language:experimental.betterFors`, at 3.6

entry .scalacEdges   the Scala compile edges of a toolchain: `Language.Scala` to each universe
  detail
      Each edge carries the `Scalac` it drives, options and all, so the compiler's own
      configuration stays where the caller wrote it and the edge's settings are empty
      (`Unit`). A toolchain holding one can start from `Deliverable.Sources`, so one path runs
      from `.scala` text to a runnable JAR with the compiler and the bundler both selected by
      the path:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val staged: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/staged".as[Path on Linux]
      val source = t"object Main:\n  def main(args: scala.Array[String]): Unit = println(\"hello\")\n"

      supervise:
        val toolchain = Toolchain(List(scalacEdges.classfile(Scalac[3.8](Nil))), jarEdges())

        toolchain.produce
          ( Deliverable.Sources(Map(t"hello.scala" -> source), classpath),
            anthology.Language.Scala,
            Artifact.Jar,
            staged,
            List(jarOptions.name(t"whole.jar")),
            List(EntryPoint(Fqcn(t"Main"))) )
      ```

      The tool, named `scalac`, creates the output directory, relays the compiler's
      `Compiler.Event`s onto the toolchain's `LinkEvent` channel, and maps the result: a
      failure raises `Link.Error` with reason `CompilationFailed(notices)` carrying every
      diagnostic, a crash `CompilerCrash`, and a compiler which cannot be configured or
      awaited `CompilerUnusable(t"scalac")`. The `Scalac` given is retargeted to the edge's
      universe, whatever it was declared for.
  refer anthology.Scalac
  refer anthology.Toolchain
  entry .classfile   the edge from `Language.Scala` to `Universe.Classfile`, driving the given `Scalac`
  entry .sjsir   the edge from `Language.Scala` to `Universe.Sjsir`, adding `-scalajs`
  entry .nir   the edge from `Language.Scala` to `Universe.Nir`; needs a `NirPlugin` given
      NIR is emitted by the Scala Native compiler plugin, so this edge exists only given
      evidence of the plugin's location, which supplies its `-Xplugin:` flag.

entry .compile   `sources.compile()`: compiles a map of named sources in the contextual `Scalac.Session`
    An extension on `Map[Text, Text]`, usable inside a `scalac.on(classpath).session: …`
    block, where the session is the contextual `Scalac.Session^`; it delegates to the
    session's own `compile`. The result is a `Scalac.Session.Process`, already complete.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import logging.silentLogging
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import systems.javaBaseSystem

    val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath

    Scalac[3.8](Nil).on(classpath).session:
      Map(t"a.scala" -> t"class A").compile().complete()
    ```

entry .compilation   the contextual `Scalac.Session` handle, inside a session block
    A `transparent inline def` returning the session in scope at its precise, scoped type,
    so that `compilation.compile(sources)` and `compilation.frontend` can be written where
    the handle has no name. Outside a `session: …` block there is no session to summon, and
    the call does not compile.

entry .CompileFlag   an enumeration with the single case `Explain`
    Declared in `anthology.scala` but consumed by nothing in the library: `-explain` is not
    among `scalacOptions`, and would be written as `Scalac.Option[Scalac.Versions](t"-explain")`.

entry .JavaVersion   a Java release, as a JDK or a JRE: `Jdk(version)` or `Jre(version)`
  detail
      `JavaVersion.Jdk(21)` or `JavaVersion.Jre(17)`, where the version is a literal of
      `.Version`, `8` to `26`. It is declared in `anthology.scala` but consumed by no option
      in the library.
  entry .Version   the Java release numbers, as literal types: `8 | 9 | … | 26`

entry .LanguageFeature   the names of Scala language features, as an enumeration
    `ClauseInterleaving`, `FewerBraces`, `RelaxedExtensionImports`, `ErasedDefinitions`,
    `SaferExceptions`, `PostfixOps`, `ImplicitConversions`, `Dynamics`, `ReflectiveCalls`,
    `CaptureChecking`, `GenericNumberLiterals`, `NamedTypeArguments`, `PureFunctions` and
    `Into`. Declared in `anthology.scala` but consumed by nothing in the library; the
    `-language:` options are the values of `scalacOptions.language.experimental`.

entry .Unused   a selection for `-Wunused`: `All`, `None`, or a `Subset` of `UnusedFeature`s
    The argument of `scalacOptions.warnings.unused`, typed by version: `All` and `None`
    exist at 3.1 to 3.8, and `Subset(features)` at 3.3 to 3.8, with its version that of the
    features it names. `Unused.None` is this enumeration's case, not vacuous's `Unset`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    scalacOptions.warnings.unused(Unused.Subset(List(UnusedFeature.Privates, UnusedFeature.Locals)))
    . flags   // List(t"-Wunused:privates,locals")
    ```

entry .UnusedFeature   one category `-Wunused` can check: imports, privates, locals, explicits, implicits, params, linted
  detail
      `Imports(strict)` exists at 3.3; `Privates`, `Locals`, `Explicits`, `Implicits`,
      `Params` and `Linted` at 3.3 to 3.6. `#name` is the word `-Wunused:` takes, and a
      `List` of them is an `Unused.Subset`.
  entry #name   the category as `-Wunused` spells it: `imports`, `privates`, `locals`, …

entry .WarningFlag   an enumeration of `Deprecation` and `Feature`
    Declared in `anthology.scala` but consumed by nothing in the library; the corresponding
    options are `scalacOptions.warnings.deprecation` and `scalacOptions.warnings.feature`.

entry .Javac   the Java compiler as a value, over the running JDK's own compiler
  detail
      `Javac(options)` drives the system Java compiler (`ToolProvider.getSystemJavaCompiler`)
      in-process, through the same shape of API as `Scalac`: a map of named sources and a
      classpath compile into a directory in a task of their own, and the result is a
      `Compiler.Process`. Java has one backend, so the output is always `Universe.Classfile`,
      and a mixed Scala and Java application links into one artifact.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import logging.silentLogging
      import probates.cancelProbate
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]

      supervise:
        val javac = Javac(List(Javac.Option(t"-Xlint:all")))
        val process = javac(classpath)(Map(t"Hello.java" -> t"class Hello {}"), out)
        process.complete()
      ```

      Java diagnostics become `Notice`s: errors as `Error`, warnings and mandatory warnings
      as `Warning`, the rest as `Info`, each with a single-line span where the compiler gives
      a position. The notice's `file` is not currently the name the source was given: it is
      the placeholder `name`, so a tool which must attribute a Java diagnostic to its source
      should read the message. Progress is two ticks, `0.1` on starting and `1.0` on
      success. The compiler is the running JDK's, so there is no version parameter and
      `.version` is the runtime's.
  refer anthology.Compiler
  refer anthology.javacEdges
  entry .refresh   replaces the shared compiler instance, as happens after a crash
  entry .compiler   the shared `javax.tools.JavaCompiler` the compiles run on
  entry .version   the running JDK's version, `Runtime.version()`: `25.0.4`
  entry .Option   a Java compiler option, as the flags it adds
    detail
        `Javac.Option(flags*)`: Java options are not version-typed, since the compiler is
        whichever JDK is running. `Javac.Option(t"--release", t"17")` or
        `Javac.Option(t"-Xlint:all")`.
    entry #flags   the command-line arguments the option contributes
  entry #options   the compiler's options, in order
  entry #version   the running JDK's version, `Javac.version`
  entry #JavaSource   a named in-memory source, as the `JavaFileObject` the compiler reads
    detail
        `javac.JavaSource(name, code)`, a `SimpleJavaFileObject` with a `string:///name` URI
        whose content is the code. `#apply` wraps each source in one; it is public for a tool
        which drives the `JavaCompiler` API directly.
    entry #name   the source's name
    entry #code   the source's content
  entry #apply   compiles named sources against a classpath into a directory, in a task of its own
      `javac(classpath)(sources, out)`: runs the system compiler with `-classpath`, `-d` and
      the options, in an `async` task, and returns the `Compiler.Process` at once. It needs a
      `System`, a `Monitor` and a `Probate`, logs `Compiler.Event`s (`Start`, `Running`, and
      `CompilerCrash` on a throw) and is declared to raise `Compiler.Error`. A crash becomes
      `Compiler.Result.Crash` and replaces the shared compiler.

entry .javacEdges   the Java compile edge of a toolchain: `Language.Java` to `Universe.Classfile`
  detail
      `javacEdges(javac)` is a one-edge list carrying the `Javac` it drives. The tool, named
      `javac`, creates the output directory, relays the compiler's events onto the
      toolchain's `LinkEvent` channel, and maps a failure to `Link.Error` reason
      `CompilationFailed(notices)`, a crash to `CompilerCrash` and an unconfigurable compiler
      to `CompilerUnusable(t"javac")`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val toolchain = Toolchain(javacEdges(Javac(Nil)), jarEdges())
      toolchain.path(anthology.Language.Java, Artifact.Jar).map(_.tool.name)   // List(t"javac", t"jar")
      ```
  entry .apply   the edge list for a `Javac`

entry .Kotlinc   the Kotlin compiler as a value: typed options and a language version, emitting classfiles
  detail
      `Kotlinc[version](options)` drives the Kotlin compiler in-process, presenting the same
      interface as `Scalac` and `Javac`: a map of named sources and a classpath compile into
      a directory, and the compiler's messages become `Notice`s on the resulting
      `Compiler.Process`. The version parameter is one of `.Versions` (`1.9` to `2.4`), and
      each `kotlincOptions` value is typed by the versions which accept it, so an option a
      version lacks is a compile error in the tool.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import logging.silentLogging
      import probates.cancelProbate
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]
      val greeting = t"package demo\n\nfun greet(): String = \"hello\"\n"

      supervise:
        val kotlinc = Kotlinc[2.4](List(kotlincOptions.warnings.asErrors, kotlincOptions.jvmTarget(17)))
        val process = kotlinc(classpath)(Map(t"demo/Greeting.kt" -> greeting), out)
        process.complete()
      ```

      Kotlin reads its sources from disk, so they are written to a scratch directory for the
      duration of the compilation, removed when it ends; a diagnostic still names the source
      as it was given (`demo/Greeting.kt`), not the file it was written to. The Kotlin
      standard library is never implied — the compiler runs with `-no-stdlib`, so
      `kotlin-stdlib` goes on the classpath like every other entry — and the output is
      classfiles, linkable as any classfile artifact, including a `Dex` or an `Apk`.

      Only what the compiler says about the sources becomes a notice: an info-level message
      with no source location (the logging `-verbose` makes copious) is dropped. The
      compiler itself (`kotlin-compiler-embeddable`) is a compile-only dependency of
      `anthology.kotlin`: the program which drives it puts the version it wants on its own
      runtime classpath, and `.version` reports which.
  refer anthology.kotlincOptions
  refer anthology.kotlincEdges
  refer anthology.Compiler
  entry .Versions   the Kotlin language versions a `Kotlinc` can be declared for: `1.9` to `2.4`
  entry .Option   a Kotlin compiler option, as the flags it adds, typed by the versions which accept it
    detail
        `Kotlinc.Option[-version <: Versions](flags*)`, contravariant in its version as
        `Scalac.Option` is, so an option declared for `Kotlinc.Versions` fits any compiler.
        An option `kotlincOptions` lacks is written directly:
        `Kotlinc.Option[Kotlinc.Versions](t"-Xcontext-receivers")`.
    entry #flags   the command-line arguments the option contributes
  entry .version   the version of the embedded compiler, as it reports itself: `2.4.10`
  entry #options   the compiler's options, in order
  entry #commandLineArguments   the flags of every option, concatenated in order
  entry #version   the version of the embedded compiler, `Kotlinc.version`
  entry #apply   compiles named sources against a classpath into a directory, in a task of its own
      `kotlinc(classpath)(sources, out)`: writes the sources to a scratch directory, runs the
      JVM compiler with `-no-stdlib`, `-classpath`, `-d` and the options, in an `async` task,
      and returns the `Compiler.Process` at once; the scratch tree is removed when the task
      ends. It needs a `System`, a `Monitor` and a `Probate`, logs `Compiler.Event`s, and is
      declared to raise `Compiler.Error`. Diagnostics carry the span the compiler reports,
      mapped back onto the name each source was given; a crash becomes
      `Compiler.Result.Crash`.

entry .kotlincOptions   the Kotlin compiler's options as typed values
  detail
      Mirrors the Kotlin command line under `kotlincOptions`, each a `Kotlinc.Option` typed
      by the versions which accept it, with nested objects `warnings`, `compatibility`,
      `explicitApi` and `advanced`. The object is mirrored as a package of the `soundness`
      umbrella.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Kotlinc[2.4](List(kotlincOptions.warnings.asErrors, kotlincOptions.jvmTarget(17)))
      . commandLineArguments   // List(t"-Werror", t"-jvm-target", t"17")

      Kotlinc[1.9](List(kotlincOptions.warnings.extra))   // does not compile
      ```
  refer anthology.Kotlinc
  entry .javaParameters   `-java-parameters`: keeps parameter names for Java reflection
  entry .noJdk   `-no-jdk`: does not put the JDK on the classpath
  entry .progressive   `-progressive`: enables progressive mode
  entry .verbose   `-verbose`: verbose compiler logging (its messages are not notices)
  entry .moduleName   `-module-name name`
  entry .jvmTarget   `-jvm-target version`: the JVM bytecode version, as a number such as `17`
  entry .optIn   `-opt-in marker`: opts in to an annotated API
  entry .warnings   `-nowarn`, `-Werror` and `-Wextra`
    detail
        `none` and `asErrors` at every version; `extra` from 2.0.
    entry .none   `-nowarn`
    entry .asErrors   `-Werror`: warnings fail the compilation
    entry .extra   `-Wextra`, at 2.0 to 2.4
  entry .compatibility   the API, language and JDK release versions to compile against
    detail
        `api` and `language` take a `Kotlinc.Versions` literal; `jdkRelease` a JDK number.
    entry .api   `-api-version v`
    entry .language   `-language-version v`
    entry .jdkRelease   `-Xjdk-release=n`
  entry .explicitApi   `-Xexplicit-api=strict` or `=warning`
    detail
        Explicit API mode, for library authors: every public declaration must state its
        visibility and return type.
    entry .strict   `-Xexplicit-api=strict`
    entry .warning   `-Xexplicit-api=warning`
  entry .advanced   `-Xno-call-assertions` and `-Xno-param-assertions`
    detail
        Drops the null-check assertions Kotlin emits at Java boundaries.
    entry .noCallAssertions   `-Xno-call-assertions`
    entry .noParameterAssertions   `-Xno-param-assertions`

entry .kotlincEdges   the Kotlin compile edge of a toolchain: `Language.Kotlin` to `Universe.Classfile`
  detail
      `kotlincEdges(kotlinc)` is a one-edge list carrying the `Kotlinc` it drives. Only the
      JVM backend is modelled: Kotlin's other backends emit klib rather than a universe
      anthology knows. The tool, named `kotlinc`, maps a failure to `Link.Error` reason
      `CompilationFailed(notices)`, a crash to `CompilerCrash` and an unconfigurable compiler
      to `CompilerUnusable(t"kotlinc")`, as `scalacEdges` does.
  entry .apply   the edge list for a `Kotlinc`

entry .Js   JavaScript bound to a host through a module system: one application node per module system
  detail
      `anthology.Js(module)` is the application node with id `js-<module>`: `js-es`,
      `js-commonjs` or `js-script`. The module system is part of the artifact's binding
      contract — how a browser or Node imports it — so it is part of the node's identity, and
      `sjsEdges()` registers one edge from `Universe.Sjsir` to each. It is **not exported** to
      the `soundness` umbrella, whose `Js` is xenophile's JavaScript foreign-function
      interface, so under `import soundness.*` it is written fully qualified.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val target = anthology.Js(anthology.Js.Module.Es)
      Toolchain(sjsEdges()).path(Universe.Sjsir, target).map(_.tool.name)   // List(t"js-es")
      Artifact.application(t"js-es") == target                               // true
      ```

      The link writes `main.js` into the output directory.
  refer anthology.sjsEdges
  refer anthology.linkerOptions
  entry .Module   how a JavaScript host consumes the artifact: `Es`, `CommonJs` or `Script`
    detail
        An ECMAScript module (`ModuleKind.ESModule`), a CommonJS module, or a plain script
        with no module system, with `#id`s `es`, `commonjs` and `script`.
    entry #id   the module system's name within the node's id
  entry #module   the module system the artifact is bound to

entry .Wasi   a standalone WebAssembly artifact bound to a version of the WASI interface: one node per version
  detail
      `Wasi(version)` is the application node whose id is the version's: `wasip1`, `wasip2`
      or `wasip3`. WASI generations are separate nodes because they determine the artifact's
      ABI, and only `Wasi(Wasi.Version.Wasip2)` — a component-model `.wasm` whose imports and
      exports are described by WIT — has an edge producing it, `sjsEdges.wasi()`, so asking
      for `Wasip3` raises `NoPath` rather than silently producing something else. The
      component edge has two prerequisites beyond the linker, both givens: a
      `WasiToolchain`, obtainable only by probing for `wasm-tools` and `wit-bindgen`, and a
      `.World` naming the WIT directory and world to link against. `ociEdges()` then wraps the
      component as an `OciImage`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]
      val linked: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/linked".as[Path on Linux]

      given WasiToolchain = WasiToolchain()
      given Wasi.World = Wasi.World(t"/home/user/project/wit".as[Path on Linux], t"my-world")

      supervise:
        Toolchain(sjsEdges(), List(sjsEdges.wasi()), ociEdges()).produce
          ( Deliverable.Emission(out, classpath), Universe.Sjsir, OciImage, linked )
      ```

      The link writes `main.wasm` into the output directory.
  refer anthology.WasiToolchain
  refer anthology.sjsEdges
  refer anthology.OciImage
  entry .Version   the WASI generations: `Wasip1`, `Wasip2`, `Wasip3`
    detail
        `wasip1` (0.1) is a flat, libc-style syscall ABI on core modules; `wasip2` (0.2) is
        the component model, with imports and exports described by WIT; `wasip3` (0.3) adds
        native asynchrony. `#id` is the lowercase name.
    entry #id   `wasip1`, `wasip2` or `wasip3`
  entry .World   the WIT definitions a component link resolves against: a directory of packages and a world name
    detail
        `Wasi.World(directory, world)`: the directory holds `.wit` packages (with their
        dependencies under `deps/`), and `world` names the world to link. `sjsEdges.wasi()`
        and `ociEdges()` both take one as a given; the OCI tool reads the world's imports and
        exports from the directory's top-level `.wit` files to describe the artifact.
    entry #directory   the directory of `.wit` packages
    entry #world   the name of the world to link
  entry #version   the WASI generation the artifact is bound to

entry .WasiToolchain   evidence that `wasm-tools` and `wit-bindgen` are installed, obtained only by probing
  detail
      A WASI component link shells out to `wasm-tools` and the scala-wasm fork of
      `wit-bindgen`; a `WasiToolchain` value is the evidence they are present, and the only
      way to make one is `.apply`, which runs each with `--version`. `sjsEdges.wasi()` takes
      one as a given, so a component edge whose tooling is absent cannot be constructed, and
      a missing tool is discovered when the toolchain is assembled rather than when a link
      fails.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      given WasiToolchain = WasiToolchain()
      ```
  entry .apply   probes for `wasm-tools` and `wit-bindgen`, raising `Toolchain.Error` if either is missing
      Runs `<tool> --version` through guillotine with the `WorkingDirectory` and
      `Environment` in scope — supply the environment the processes should inherit
      (`environments.javaBaseEnvironment`, or a CLI's own) — and raises `Toolchain.Error`
      naming the first tool which does not exit successfully.

entry .Wasm   a core WebAssembly module with JavaScript glue, bound to a JavaScript host
    `anthology.Wasm` is the application node with id `wasm`, reached from `Universe.Sjsir`
    through `sjsEdges()`: the Scala.js linker's WebAssembly backend, emitted as an ES2022
    module which a browser or Node loads with its glue. It is **not exported** to the
    `soundness` umbrella, whose `Wasm` is xenophile's, so it is written fully qualified. The
    link writes `main.wasm` into the output directory, and needs no native tooling.

entry .linkerOptions   settings for every format the Scala.js linker produces: IR checks, source maps, ES version, optimizer
  detail
      Each is a `Toolchain.Setting` addressed to `anthology.Js` (any module system),
      `anthology.Wasm` and `Wasi` alike, editing the linker's `StandardConfig`; on a path
      producing none of those it is rejected as `InapplicableSetting`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val settings = List(linkerOptions.optimize.fast, linkerOptions.esVersion.es2020, linkerOptions.sourceMaps)
      ```
  entry .checkIr   checks the IR before linking
  entry .sourceMaps   emits source maps
  entry .esVersion   the ECMAScript version to emit: `es2015` to `es2022`
    detail
        One setting per version, `linkerOptions.esVersion.es2020` for example.
    entry .es2015   targets ECMAScript 2015
    entry .es2016   targets ECMAScript 2016
    entry .es2017   targets ECMAScript 2017
    entry .es2018   targets ECMAScript 2018
    entry .es2019   targets ECMAScript 2019
    entry .es2020   targets ECMAScript 2020
    entry .es2021   targets ECMAScript 2021
    entry .es2022   targets ECMAScript 2022
  entry .optimize   whether the linker's optimizer runs: `none` or `fast`
    detail
        `fast` enables the optimizer, `none` disables it.
    entry .none   disables the optimizer
    entry .fast   enables the optimizer

entry .sjsEdges   the Scala.js link edges: `Sjsir` to each JavaScript module system, to browser Wasm, and to a WASI component
  detail
      `sjsEdges()` registers four edges from `Universe.Sjsir`: to `anthology.Js` under the
      ES, CommonJS and script module systems, and to `anthology.Wasm`; `.wasi()` adds the
      fifth, to `Wasi(Wasi.Version.Wasip2)`, and exists only given a `WasiToolchain` and a
      `Wasi.World`. All drive the Scala.js linker — a JVM library bundled with
      `anthology.link` — over the emission directory and its classpath's directories and
      JARs, with each `EntryPoint` as a module initializer calling its `main`, and write
      `main.js` or `main.wasm` into the output directory. The linker's messages are logged as
      `LinkEvent.Message`s, and a linker which throws raises `Link.Error` reason `Failed`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]
      val linked: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/linked".as[Path on Linux]

      supervise:
        Toolchain(sjsEdges()).produce
          ( Deliverable.Emission(out, classpath),
            Universe.Sjsir,
            anthology.Js(anthology.Js.Module.Es),
            linked,
            List(linkerOptions.optimize.fast),
            List(EntryPoint(Fqcn(t"Main"))) )
      ```

      The emission must have been compiled with `-scalajs` (a `Scalac` retargeted to
      `Universe.Sjsir`) against the Scala.js runtime JARs; a classfile compilation has no
      path to any of these nodes.
  refer anthology.linkerOptions
  refer anthology.Wasi
  entry .apply   the four edges needing no native tooling: three JavaScript module systems and browser Wasm
      The tool of each is named by its node's id (`js-es`, `js-commonjs`, `js-script`,
      `wasm`), and the Wasm edge fixes ES2022 and the WebAssembly backend.
  entry .wasi   the edge to `Wasi(Wasip2)`, given a probed `WasiToolchain` and a `Wasi.World`
      Configures the linker for a `WasmComponent` module against the world's WIT directory
      and world name; the link runs `wasm-tools` and `wit-bindgen` as it goes. A WASI link
      whose native tooling is absent is not expressible, since the given cannot be made.

entry .Bundler   the running application's classpath, for a build which must package itself
  detail
      Its one public member, `.applicationClasspath`, is the classpath the running program
      was loaded from. A staging rig which must fold its own running classpath into the JARs
      it produces pairs it with its compiled output as a `Deliverable.Emission` and packages
      the two through the `Artifact.Jar` edge, so the JAR carries the rig's own executor and
      dependencies:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      def stage(out: Path on Linux, executor: Fqcn)(using Monitor): Path on Linux =
        Toolchain(jarEdges()).produce
          ( Deliverable.Emission(out, Bundler.applicationClasspath),
            Universe.Classfile,
            Artifact.Jar,
            out,
            List(jarOptions.name(t"staged.jar")),
            List(EntryPoint(executor)) )
      ```

      The JAR assembly itself is not public: it is what the `jarEdges` tools run.
  entry .applicationClasspath   the classpath of the running application, read from its own classloader
      `LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Bundler.type])`: introspected from the classloader which
      loaded this class — its own, rather than the thread's context loader, which a pooled
      thread may have inherited from whatever ran first — falling back to the
      `java.class.path` property. Under a host such as a test runner, which isolates code in
      a `URLClassLoader`, this is the classpath the code actually came from.

entry .Library   a JAR of one universe's unlinked compilation output, for downstream assembly: one node per universe
  detail
      `Library(universe)` is the application node with id `library-<universe>`
      (`library-classfile`, `library-sjsir`, `library-nir`), reached from its universe
      through `jarEdges()`. It packages only the compilation's own output — classfiles, and
      any `.sjsir` or `.nir` alongside them, with their TASTy — not the classpath, and
      records no entry point, so entry points are ignored. A library's universe must match
      its compilation's: `Toolchain(jarEdges()).path(Universe.Sjsir, Library(Universe.Nir))`
      raises `NoPath`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]
      val linked: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/linked".as[Path on Linux]

      supervise:
        Toolchain(jarEdges()).produce
          ( Deliverable.Emission(out, classpath), Universe.Sjsir, Library(Universe.Sjsir), linked )
      ```

      The JAR is named `main.jar` unless `jarOptions.name` says otherwise.
  refer anthology.jarEdges
  entry #universe   the universe whose output the library packages

entry .jarOptions   settings for the JAR-packaging edges
  detail
      Only the filename: `jarOptions.name(t"app.jar")`, addressed to `Artifact.Jar` and
      every `Library` node alike.
  entry .name   the JAR's filename within the output directory, for `Artifact.Jar` and `Library` nodes
      The default is `main.jar`.

entry .jarEdges   the JAR-packaging edges: `Classfile` to `Artifact.Jar`, and each universe to its `Library`
  detail
      `jarEdges()` registers four edges, needing no external tool: from `Universe.Classfile`
      to `Artifact.Jar`, and from each of `Universe.Classfile`, `Universe.Sjsir` and
      `Universe.Nir` to its `Library`. Both tools' settings are the JAR's filename
      (`jarOptions.name`, default `main.jar`).

      The `jar` tool bundles the emission directory *and the whole classpath*: a directory
      entry's files are added (omitting any `MANIFEST.MF` and `plugin.properties`), a JAR
      entry's entries are re-emitted verbatim without recompression (omitting its manifest),
      and duplicates by path are dropped, the first occurrence winning. The manifest records
      `Created-By: Soundness` and, from the single `EntryPoint`, `Main-Class`; no entry point
      gives a JAR without one, and two raise `Link.Error` reason `ManyEntryPoints`. The
      `library` tool bundles only the emission directory and ignores entry points. A zip,
      path, I/O, truncation or filename fault is `Link.Error` reason `Packaging(detail)`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]
      val linked: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/linked".as[Path on Linux]

      supervise:
        val jar: Path on Linux =
          Toolchain(jarEdges()).produce
            ( Deliverable.Emission(out, classpath),
              Universe.Classfile,
              Artifact.Jar,
              linked,
              List(jarOptions.name(t"app.jar")),
              List(EntryPoint(Fqcn(t"Main"))) )

        sh"java -jar $jar".exec[Text]()
      ```
  refer anthology.Library
  refer anthology.jarOptions
  entry .apply   the four packaging edges

entry .nativeOptions   settings for native binaries: garbage collector, build mode and link-time optimization
  detail
      Each is a `Toolchain.Setting` addressed to every `Artifact.Binary` node, editing Scala
      Native's `NativeConfig`; on a path producing no binary it is rejected as
      `InapplicableSetting`. The defaults are `gc.immix`, `mode.debug` and `lto.none`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val release = List(nativeOptions.mode.releaseFast, nativeOptions.gc.commix, nativeOptions.lto.thin)
      ```
  entry .gc   the garbage collector: `immix`, `commix`, `boehm` or `none`
    detail
        Immix is the default; Commix its concurrent variant; Boehm the conservative
        collector; `none` allocates without collecting.
    entry .immix   the Immix collector (the default)
    entry .commix   the concurrent Commix collector
    entry .boehm   the Boehm conservative collector
    entry .none   no garbage collection
  entry .mode   the build mode: `debug`, `releaseFast` or `releaseFull`
    detail
        `debug` is the default and quickest to link; the release modes optimise, `releaseFull`
        most aggressively.
    entry .debug   a debug build (the default)
    entry .releaseFast   an optimised build, favouring link time
    entry .releaseFull   a fully optimised build
  entry .lto   link-time optimization: `none`, `thin` or `full`
    detail
        `none` is the default.
    entry .none   no link-time optimization (the default)
    entry .thin   ThinLTO
    entry .full   full LTO

entry .nativeEdges   the native link edges: `Nir` to an `Artifact.Binary` per target triple, through `clang`
  detail
      `nativeEdges(triples*)` probes once for `clang` and `clang++` on the `PATH` (with the
      `WorkingDirectory` and `Environment` in scope), raising `Toolchain.Error` if either is
      missing, and registers one edge from `Universe.Nir` to `Artifact.Binary(triple)` for
      each triple given — or for the build host's own, `Triple.host`, when none is. The tool
      drives the Scala Native build (bundled with `anthology.nir`) over the emission
      directory and its classpath, requires exactly one `EntryPoint` as the binary's main
      class (`NoEntryPoint` otherwise), and writes the binary, named `main`, below the
      output directory. Targets beyond the build host require a C toolchain and sysroot
      capable of cross-compiling to them.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]
      val linked: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/linked".as[Path on Linux]

      supervise:
        Toolchain(nativeEdges(Triple.Arm64MacOs, Triple.X64Linux)).produce
          ( Deliverable.Emission(out, classpath),
            Universe.Nir,
            Artifact.Binary(Triple.X64Linux),
            linked,
            List(nativeOptions.mode.releaseFast, nativeOptions.gc.commix),
            List(EntryPoint(Fqcn(t"Main"))) )
      ```

      The emission must have been compiled into `Universe.Nir` — a `Scalac` retargeted with
      a `NirPlugin` given — against the Scala Native runtime JARs. The compiler's own JARs
      are compile-classpath only and unreachable at native run time, which is why code
      which uses the compiler only inside macros still links. A build which throws raises
      `Link.Error` reason `Failed`.
  refer anthology.nativeOptions
  refer anthology.Triple
  refer anthology.NirPlugin
  entry .apply   probes for `clang` and `clang++`, then one edge per triple (or the host's)
      Raises `Toolchain.Error(t"clang")` for a missing compiler, and the same for a build host
      whose triple is unrecognised when no triples are named.

entry .Dex   Dalvik bytecode bound to the Android runtime: an archive of `classes*.dex` files
  detail
      The application node with id `dex`, reached from `Universe.Classfile` through
      `dexEdges()`: a zip archive, `main.dex.jar`, of `classes.dex`, `classes2.dex`, …, which
      ART and a `DexClassLoader` consume directly, and the intermediate node on the path from
      classfiles to an `Apk`. DEX carries no entry-point metadata — ART is told its main class
      at invocation — so the edge ignores entry points.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]
      val linked: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/linked".as[Path on Linux]

      supervise:
        Toolchain(dexEdges()).produce(Deliverable.Emission(out, classpath), Universe.Classfile, Dex, linked)
      ```

      Scala 3 output dexes cleanly: the compiler emits Java 17 class files with lambdas as
      `invokedynamic` but no `ConstantDynamic` and no nest attributes, so D8's weakest
      desugaring areas are untouched, and API level 26 needs no platform stubs. Libraries
      which need Panama or `java.net.http` have no Android runtime to run on, dexed or not.
  refer anthology.dexEdges
  refer anthology.dexOptions
  refer anthology.Apk

entry .dexOptions   settings for dexing: the minimum API level, the platform stubs, and D8's mode
  detail
      Each is a `Toolchain.Setting` addressed to the `Dex` node. The defaults are API level
      26, debug mode and no platform stubs. `apkOptions.minApi` sets the level on `Dex` and
      `Apk` together, which an APK build should prefer.
  entry .minApi   the lowest Android API level the output must run on (default 26)
      Lower levels oblige D8 to desugar more. Levels below 24 lack native default interface
      methods, which Scala 3 emits for trait members, so they additionally demand `.platform`
      to desugar against.
  entry .platform   an Android SDK's platform stubs, `platforms/android-<n>/android.jar`
      Needed only below API level 24, or when the program references platform types.
  entry .mode   D8's compilation mode: `debug` or `release`
    detail
        `debug` is the default.
    entry .debug   `CompilationMode.DEBUG`
    entry .release   `CompilationMode.RELEASE`

entry .dexEdges   the dexing edge: `Classfile` to `Dex`, driving R8's D8 through its API
  detail
      `dexEdges()` is a one-edge list. The tool, named `dex`, feeds D8 (a JVM library bundled
      with `anthology.dex`) the emission directory's classfiles and the whole classpath —
      archives wholesale, directories as their enumerated `.class` files, with
      `module-info.class` excluded — and writes `main.dex.jar` into the output directory.
      D8's errors and warnings are logged as `LinkEvent.Message`s at `warn`; a run which
      throws raises `Link.Error` reason `Failed`.
  entry .apply   the dexing edge

entry .Apk   a complete, installable Android package: dexed code, a binary manifest, aligned and signed
  detail
      The application node with id `apk`, reached from `Universe.Classfile` through `Dex` by
      `Toolchain(dexEdges(), apkEdges())`. The tool encodes a binary `AndroidManifest.xml`
      (`Axml`), stores the dex files uncompressed and 4-byte aligned so the runtime can
      memory-map them, and signs the package with APK Signature Scheme v2 (`.Signer`) — a
      debuggable, emulator-installable `app.apk`, produced with no Android SDK build tool,
      only a keystore. Exactly one `EntryPoint` is required: its class is the launcher
      activity.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import environments.javaBaseEnvironment
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import systems.javaBaseSystem
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import workingDirectories.javaBaseWorkingDirectory

      val classpath: LocalClasspath = Bundler.applicationClasspath
      val out: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/out".as[Path on Linux]
      val staged: Path on Linux = t"/tmp/staged".as[Path on Linux]

      supervise:
        Toolchain(dexEdges(), apkEdges()).produce
          ( Deliverable.Emission(out, classpath),
            Universe.Classfile,
            Apk,
            staged,
            List
              ( apkOptions.packageName(t"com.example.app"),
                apkOptions.label(t"Example"),
                apkOptions.version(3, t"1.2"),
                apkOptions.permission(t"android.permission.INTERNET") ),
            List(EntryPoint(Fqcn(t"com.example.MainActivity"))) )
      ```

      The configuration's defaults (`Apk.Configuration.default`) sign with the conventional
      Android debug keystore, `~/.android/debug.keystore`, which Android Studio shares; a
      release build names its own with `apkOptions.keystore`. The dex archive lands in
      `staged/dex/` and the package in `staged/app.apk`, alongside the `unsigned.apk` it was
      signed from.
  refer anthology.apkEdges
  refer anthology.apkOptions
  refer anthology.Dex
  refer anthology.Axml
  entry .Configuration   what a package needs beyond its code: API levels, names, permissions and the signing key
    detail
        The settings type of the APK tool, which `apkOptions` edits: the API level range,
        the package name and human label, the version code and name, the requested runtime
        permissions, and the PKCS#12 keystore, its password, the key's alias and the key's
        password. The launcher activity is not here: it is the path's `EntryPoint`.
    entry .default   the defaults: API 26 to 34, `dev.soundness.app`, version 1 `1.0`, the Android debug keystore
        `minApi` 26, `targetApi` 34, package `dev.soundness.app`, version code 1 and name
        `1.0`, label `Soundness App`, no permissions, and the debug keystore
        `~/.android/debug.keystore` with store password `android`, alias `androiddebugkey`
        and key password `android`.
    entry #minApi   the lowest Android API level the application runs on
    entry #targetApi   the API level the application targets
    entry #packageName   the Android package name
    entry #versionCode   the integer version code
    entry #versionName   the human-readable version
    entry #label   the application's label
    entry #permissions   the runtime permissions requested, by name
    entry #keystore   the path of the PKCS#12 keystore to sign with
    entry #storePass   the keystore's password
    entry #alias   the alias of the signing key within the keystore
    entry #keyPass   the signing key's password
  entry .Manifest   builds the `Axml.Element` tree of an Android manifest
    detail
        `Apk.Manifest(packageName, versionCode, versionName, minSdk, targetSdk, label,
        activity, permissions)` returns the `manifest` element — `uses-sdk`, one
        `uses-permission` per permission, and an `application` holding the exported launcher
        `activity` with its `MAIN`/`LAUNCHER` intent filter — ready for `Axml.encode`. The
        attribute resource IDs are the stable public `android.R.attr` constants, so no
        platform `android.jar` is consulted.
    entry .apply   the manifest element for the given package, versions, API levels, label, activity and permissions
  entry .Signer   APK Signature Scheme v2, over a finished unsigned package
    detail
        Signs an aligned, unsigned ZIP by inserting an APK Signing Block between its entries
        and its central directory; the signature covers a SHA-256 digest of the whole file in
        1 MiB chunks, so any later byte change invalidates it, which is why signing is the
        last step. The RSA signature (`SHA256withRSA`), keystore and certificate use
        `java.security` directly, so the key must be an RSA key in a PKCS#12 store. No
        Android SDK tool is involved: this replaces `apksigner`.
    entry .sign   signs the unsigned package's bytes with the key and certificate from a keystore
        `Apk.Signer.sign(unsigned, keystore, storePass, alias, keyPass)` returns the signed
        package's bytes.

entry .Axml   Android binary XML: the encoding of `AndroidManifest.xml` inside a package
  detail
      Android reads a package's manifest as binary XML, with a string pool, a resource map
      and typed attribute values, not as text. `Axml` models a document as `.Element`s with
      `.Attribute`s whose values are a `.Value` — a string, an integer or a boolean, since
      Android reads each attribute at a specific type — and `.encode` writes the binary form.
      `Apk.Manifest` builds the tree an application needs; `Axml` is public for a manifest
      which needs more.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val manifest =
        Axml.Element
          ( t"manifest",
            List(Axml.Attribute(Unset, t"package", Unset, Axml.Value.Str(t"com.example.app"))),
            Nil )

      val bytes: Data = Axml.encode(manifest)
      ```
  refer anthology.Apk
  entry .androidUri   the Android resources namespace, `http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android`
  entry .androidPrefix   the prefix a manifest conventionally binds the namespace to, `android`
  entry .Value   a typed attribute value: `Str(text)`, `Num(value)` or `Bool(flag)`
      Android reads each attribute at a specific `TypedValue` type, so the encoder commits to
      one rather than emitting every value as a string: a string references the pool, an
      integer or boolean is stored inline.
  entry .Attribute   an attribute: its namespace, local name, public resource ID and typed value
    detail
        `Axml.Attribute(namespace, name, resourceId, value)`: the namespace is `.androidUri`
        for an `android:` attribute or `Unset` for an unnamespaced one such as `package`; the
        resource ID is the public `android.R.attr` constant Android identifies the attribute
        by, or `Unset`.
    entry #namespace   the namespace URI, or `Unset`
    entry #name   the local name
    entry #resourceId   the public resource ID, or `Unset`
    entry #value   the typed value
  entry .Element   an element: its name, attributes and children
    detail
        `Axml.Element(name, attributes, children)`.
    entry #name   the element's name
    entry #attributes   the element's attributes, in order
    entry #children   the child elements, in order
  entry .encode   the binary XML document for an element tree, as `Data`
      Writes the chunk header, the string pool (every name, namespace and string value,
      interned in order of first use), the resource map of the attributes' IDs, the
      `android` namespace declaration and the element tree, each chunk 4-byte aligned.

entry .apkOptions   settings for an Android package: API levels, package name, label, version, permissions, keystore
  detail
      Each is a `Toolchain.Setting` editing `Apk.Configuration` on the `Apk` node; `.minApi`
      alone addresses `Dex` as well, since the level governs both how D8 desugars and what
      the manifest declares. Dex settings other than the level (`dexOptions.mode`,
      `dexOptions.platform`) are addressed to the `Dex` node.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val settings =
        List
          ( apkOptions.minApi(26),
            apkOptions.targetApi(34),
            apkOptions.packageName(t"com.example.app"),
            apkOptions.keystore(t"/keys/release.p12", t"secret", t"release", t"secret") )
      ```
  entry .minApi   the lowest API level, applied to both `Dex` and `Apk`
      Dispatches on the format it is applied to: the `DexConfiguration` on `Dex`, the
      `Apk.Configuration` on `Apk`.
  entry .targetApi   the API level the application targets
  entry .packageName   the Android package name
  entry .label   the application's human label
  entry .version   the version code and version name, together
  entry .permission   adds a requested runtime permission, such as `android.permission.VIBRATE`
      Repeatable: each call appends one.
  entry .keystore   the PKCS#12 keystore path, its password, the key alias and the key password

entry .apkEdges   the packaging edge: `Dex` to `Apk`
  detail
      `apkEdges()` is a one-edge list, consuming the dex archive the `Dex` edge produced
      before it on the path, so `Toolchain(dexEdges(), apkEdges())` is the usual pairing.
      The tool, named `apk`, requires exactly one `EntryPoint` (`NoEntryPoint` or
      `ManyEntryPoints` otherwise), encodes the manifest, stores each `classes*.dex` from the
      archive uncompressed and 4-byte aligned, writes `unsigned.apk`, signs it, and writes
      `app.apk`. A dex archive which does not read, or a package which does not write, is
      `Link.Error` reason `Packaging(detail)`.
  entry .apply   the packaging edge

entry .OciConfiguration   the metadata written into an OCI artifact's config: architecture, OS, author, annotations
  detail
      The settings type of the OCI tool, which `ociOptions` edits; the defaults are
      architecture `wasm`, OS `wasip2`, no author and no annotations. `#os` names the WASI
      generation the component was built for: it is the field a container runtime
      dispatches on, and it moves with WASI — `wasip1` for the preview-1 syscall ABI,
      `wasip2` for the component model, `wasip3` once native async lands.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val config = OciConfiguration()
      (config.architecture, config.os)   // (t"wasm", t"wasip2")
      ```
  entry #architecture   the platform architecture, `wasm` by default
  entry #os   the platform OS, the WASI generation: `wasip2` by default
  entry #author   the artifact's author, or `Unset`
  entry #annotations   OCI annotations, as a map

entry .OciImage   a WASI component packaged as a Wasm OCI artifact, written as an `oci-archive`
    The application node with id `oci`, reached from `Universe.Sjsir` through the `wasip2`
    component by `Toolchain(sjsEdges(), List(sjsEdges.wasi()), ociEdges())`: the form a
    container runtime with a Wasm shim (`io.containerd.wasmtime.v1`) schedules and a
    registry distributes. The tool writes `image.tar` into the output directory, with the
    component in `linked/wasip2/` beside it. Its `component` metadata — the interfaces the
    workload imports and exports — is read from the `Wasi.World` the component was linked
    against, not from the binary; a world which exports `wasi:http/incoming-handler@0.2.0`
    is marked with the target `wasi:http/proxy@0.2.0`, which is what lets a host know the
    component is servable.

entry .ociOptions   settings for an OCI image: architecture, OS, author and annotations
  detail
      Each is a `Toolchain.Setting` editing `OciConfiguration` on the `OciImage` node.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val settings =
        List
          ( ociOptions.author(t"Example Ltd"),
            ociOptions.annotation(t"org.opencontainers.image.version", t"1.2.0") )
      ```
  entry .architecture   the platform architecture (default `wasm`)
  entry .os   the platform OS, the WASI generation (default `wasip2`)
  entry .author   the artifact's author
  entry .annotation   adds one OCI annotation, by key and value
      Repeatable: each call defines one more key.

entry .ociEdges   the wrapping edge: the `wasip2` component to its `OciImage`
  detail
      `ociEdges()` is a one-edge list from `Wasi(Wasi.Version.Wasip2)` to `OciImage`, taking
      the `Wasi.World` as a given. The tool, named `oci`, consumes the component the
      preceding edge (`sjsEdges.wasi()`) linked, builds the image through embarcadero's
      `Image.wasm` with the world's imports and exports read from the top-level `.wit` files
      of the world's directory (only that level is searched: `deps/` holds the packages the
      world draws on, and none declares the world itself; a world that cannot be found
      contributes no interfaces rather than failing), and writes `image.tar`. The edge
      itself needs only the world — it is the component edge whose provider probes the WASI
      toolchain — so its graph shape can be checked without `wasm-tools` installed. A write
      which throws raises `Link.Error` reason `Failed`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      given Wasi.World = Wasi.World(t"/home/user/project/wit".as[Path on Linux], t"main")
      val toolchain = Toolchain(ociEdges())
      safely(toolchain.path(Universe.Sjsir, OciImage))   // Unset: the component edge is missing
      ```
  refer anthology.OciImage
  refer anthology.Wasi
  entry .apply   the wrapping edge, given a `Wasi.World`
